QB.add(16,[{"id":16521,"t":"Dermis","s":"In the cold, contraction of structure <b>A</b> makes the hairs stand on end. Name it.","a":["arrector pili","arrector pili muscle","arrectores pilorum","arrector pilli"],"e":"A is the arrector pili muscle, part of the pilosebaceous unit. Its contraction causes piloerection: air trapped next to the skin insulates (more important in animals).","k":"A = arrector pili.","img":{"src":"16/o_skin","cap":"Structure of the skin. Image: OpenStax College : J. Gordon Betts, Peter Desaix, Eddie Johnson., CC BY 3.0, Wikimedia Commons; labels removed."},"rn":[["","Arrector Pili","Arrector Pili","Small muscle in the dermis attached to hair follicles; Contraction makes hairs stand on end"],["","What are the 3 parts of the pilosebaceous unit?","What are the 3 parts of the pilosebaceous unit?","Hair follicles; Sebaceous glands; Arrector pili muscle"]]},{"id":16522,"t":"Dermis","s":"Gland <b>B</b> is made of foamy, lipid-filled cells. How does its secretion reach the skin surface?","o":["Through the sebaceous duct into the hair follicle at its isthmus","Through its own duct opening directly onto the skin as a pore (acrosyringium)","By diffusion up through the stratum corneum","By drainage into the dermal lymphatics","Through the duct of the neighbouring eccrine sweat gland"],"w":["The acrosyringium is the opening of a sweat duct, not a sebaceous gland.","The stratum corneum is a barrier, not a route for sebum.","Lymphatics drain tissue fluid, not glandular secretions to the surface.","Sebaceous and eccrine glands drain separately."],"e":"B is a sebaceous gland, formed from sebocytes that look foamy because of lipid secretions. It is a holocrine gland producing sebum, drains into the hair follicle via the sebaceous duct at the isthmus, and several glands can serve one follicle.","k":"Sebaceous gland → follicle isthmus.","img":{"src":"16/o_skin","cap":"Structure of the skin. Image: OpenStax College : J. Gordon Betts, Peter Desaix, Eddie Johnson., CC BY 3.0, Wikimedia Commons; labels removed."},"rn":[["","Where do sebaceous glands drain?","Where do sebaceous glands drain?","Into the hair follicle; Via the sebaceous duct at the isthmus"]]},{"id":16523,"t":"Dermis","s":"Gland <b>C</b> is found all over the body. Which description matches it?","o":["Cuboidal cells surrounded by myoepithelial cells; responsible for temperature control","Columnar cells with “decapitation” secretion, found mainly in the axilla, groin and nipple, causing body odour","Foamy sebocytes producing sebum that drains into the hair follicle","Holocrine gland attached to the arrector pili muscle","Spindle-shaped cells that synthesise collagen and extracellular matrix"],"w":["That is an apocrine gland, found only in specific sites.","That is a sebaceous gland (B).","Sebaceous glands are holocrine; C is a coiled sweat gland.","Those are fibroblasts."],"e":"C is an eccrine sweat gland: found all over the body, cuboidal cells surrounded by myoepithelial cells, responsible for temperature control (evaporative cooling, latent heat of vaporisation). Apocrine glands are in the axilla, groin and nipple, with columnar cells and decapitation secretion, and cause body odour.","k":"Eccrine = everywhere, temperature.","img":{"src":"16/o_skin","cap":"Structure of the skin. Image: OpenStax College : J. Gordon Betts, Peter Desaix, Eddie Johnson., CC BY 3.0, Wikimedia Commons; labels removed."},"rn":[["","What cells make eccrine glands?","What cells make eccrine glands?","Cuboidal cells; Surrounded by myoepithelial cells"],["","What are eccrine glands responsible for?","What are eccrine glands responsible for?","Temperature control"]]},{"id":16524,"t":"Introduction","s":"A patient has a firm, indurated skin lesion. Induration places the disease process mainly in which labelled layer?","o":["H","G","F","E","D"],"w":["G is the epidermis: blistering (bullous) lesions point there.","F is the subcutis (fat).","E is the hair root inside the follicle.","D is a sensory receptor, not a layer."],"e":"Thinking structurally: induration points to the dermis (H); blistering/bullous lesions point to the epidermis (G). The dermis is dense connective tissue (collagen, elastin, extracellular matrix) supporting the epidermis.","k":"Induration = dermis; blisters = epidermis.","img":{"src":"16/o_skin","cap":"Structure of the skin. Image: OpenStax College : J. Gordon Betts, Peter Desaix, Eddie Johnson., CC BY 3.0, Wikimedia Commons; labels removed."},"rn":[["","Induration is a problem of which skin layer?","Induration is a problem of which skin layer?","Dermis"],["","Blistering (bullous) lesions are a problem of which skin layer?","Blistering (bullous) lesions are a problem of which skin layer?","Epidermis"],["","What does the dermis do?","What does the dermis do?","Supports the epidermis"]]},{"id":16525,"t":"Introduction","s":"A patient presents with widespread blisters (bullous lesions). Which named layer, labelled <b>G</b>, do such lesions point to?","a":["epidermis","the epidermis"],"e":"G is the epidermis, a keratinising stratified squamous epithelium. Blistering/bullous lesions point to the epidermis; induration points to the dermis.","k":"Blisters → epidermis.","img":{"src":"16/o_skin","cap":"Structure of the skin. Image: OpenStax College : J. Gordon Betts, Peter Desaix, Eddie Johnson., CC BY 3.0, Wikimedia Commons; labels removed."},"rn":[["","Blistering (bullous) lesions are a problem of which skin layer?","Blistering (bullous) lesions are a problem of which skin layer?","Epidermis"]]},{"id":16526,"t":"Structure of the Skin","s":"Name the fat layer labelled <b>F</b>, lying beneath the dermis.","a":["subcutis","sub cutis","sub-cutis","hypodermis","subcutaneous fat","subcutaneous tissue","subcutaneous layer"],"e":"F is the subcutis: the fat layer underneath the dermis (adipocytes).","k":"F = subcutis.","img":{"src":"16/o_skin","cap":"Structure of the skin. Image: OpenStax College : J. Gordon Betts, Peter Desaix, Eddie Johnson., CC BY 3.0, Wikimedia Commons; labels removed."}},{"id":16527,"t":"Epidermis","s":"Which labelling of layers A to E is correct?","o":["A corneum; B lucidum; C granulosum; D spinosum; E basale","A corneum; B granulosum; C lucidum; D spinosum; E basale","A lucidum; B corneum; C granulosum; D basale; E spinosum","A corneum; B lucidum; C spinosum; D granulosum; E basale","A granulosum; B lucidum; C corneum; D spinosum; E basale"],"w":["The lucidum lies directly under the corneum, above the granulosum.","The corneum is the outermost, anucleate layer.","The granular layer (1–3 cells) lies above the thick prickle layer.","Corneum is the surface; granulosum lies deeper."],"e":"Surface to base: stratum corneum (horn cells, no nucleus, basket-weave keratin), stratum lucidum (acral skin only), stratum granulosum (1–3 layers, keratohyaline granules), stratum spinosum (5–10 cells, main bulk), stratum basale (single layer on the basement membrane).","k":"Come, Let’s Get Sun Burnt.","img":{"src":"16/o_epidermis","cap":"Layers of the epidermis (thick skin). Image: OpenStax College : J. Gordon Betts, Peter Desaix, Eddie Johnson., CC BY 3.0, Wikimedia Commons; labels removed."},"rn":[["","What are the 4 layers of the epidermis (surface to deep)?","What are the 4 layers of the epidermis (surface to deep)?","Stratum corneum (horn cell); Stratum granulosum (granular cell); Stratum spinosum (prickle cell); Stratum basale (basal cell)"],["","Image card: Name the 4 layers marked by the arrows, top to bottom.","Name the 4 layers marked by the arrows, top to bottom.","Horn cell layer (stratum corneum); Granular cell layer (stratum granulosum); Prickle cell layer (stratum spinosum); Basal cell layer (stratum basale)"]]},{"id":16528,"t":"Epidermis","s":"Layer <b>B</b> is clearly seen here but is absent in a biopsy from the forearm. Why?","o":["It is a clear layer of dead keratinocytes found only at acral sites such as the palms and soles","It is lost from the forearm by sun exposure","It is only formed after skin has healed from a wound","It contains melanocytes, which are absent from the forearm","It is only visible with special stains, not H&amp;E"],"w":["UV exposure is not what defines this layer.","It is a normal layer at acral sites.","Melanocytes sit in the basal layer.","This is about site, not stain."],"e":"B is the stratum lucidum: a clear layer of dead keratinocytes found only in acral sites (palms and soles), which therefore have a thicker keratin layer.","k":"Lucidum = palms and soles only.","img":{"src":"16/o_epidermis","cap":"Layers of the epidermis (thick skin). Image: OpenStax College : J. Gordon Betts, Peter Desaix, Eddie Johnson., CC BY 3.0, Wikimedia Commons; labels removed."},"rn":[["","What is the stratum lucidum?","What is the stratum lucidum?","Clear layer of dead keratinocytes"],["","Acral","Acral","Palms and soles"]]},{"id":16529,"t":"Epidermis","s":"Name layer <b>D</b>, 5–10 cells thick, whose pink polyhedral cells form the main bulk of the epidermis.","a":["stratum spinosum","spinosum","prickle cell layer","prickle layer","prickle cell"],"e":"D is the stratum spinosum (prickle cell layer). The “prickles” are desmosomes, seen well when the epidermis is spongiotic.","k":"D = spinosum.","img":{"src":"16/o_epidermis","cap":"Layers of the epidermis (thick skin). Image: OpenStax College : J. Gordon Betts, Peter Desaix, Eddie Johnson., CC BY 3.0, Wikimedia Commons; labels removed."},"rn":[["","How thick is the stratum spinosum?","How thick is the stratum spinosum?","5 to 10 cells thick; Main bulk of the epidermis"]]},{"id":16530,"t":"Skin Cancer","s":"Breslow thickness, which sets melanoma prognosis, is measured from which labelled layer to the deepest point of the tumour?","o":["C","A","B","D","E"],"w":["A is the stratum corneum.","B is the stratum lucidum.","D is the stratum spinosum.","E is the stratum basale."],"e":"Breslow thickness is measured in mm from the granular cell layer (C, stratum granulosum) to the deepest point of the tumour. &lt;1 mm: small metastatic risk, excellent prognosis; &gt;4 mm: high risk, poor prognosis.","k":"Breslow starts at the granular layer.","img":{"src":"16/o_epidermis","cap":"Layers of the epidermis (thick skin). Image: OpenStax College : J. Gordon Betts, Peter Desaix, Eddie Johnson., CC BY 3.0, Wikimedia Commons; labels removed."},"rn":[["","Breslow Thickness","Breslow Thickness","From granular cell layer to deepest point of tumour; Measured in mm"]]},{"id":16531,"t":"Epidermis","s":"Cell <b>F</b>, a dendritic cell from the neural crest, passes melanosomes to neighbouring basal cells. Name it.","a":["melanocyte","melanocytes"],"e":"F is a melanocyte: neural-crest derived, not epithelial, in the basal layer at about 1 in 4 basal cells, seen as an oval nucleus with a clear halo. Its melanin absorbs harmful UV.","k":"F = melanocyte.","img":{"src":"16/o_epidermis","cap":"Layers of the epidermis (thick skin). Image: OpenStax College : J. Gordon Betts, Peter Desaix, Eddie Johnson., CC BY 3.0, Wikimedia Commons; labels removed."},"rn":[["","What is the ratio of melanocytes to basal cells?","What is the ratio of melanocytes to basal cells?","1 melanocyte per 4 basal cells (1:4)"]]},{"id":16532,"t":"Epidermis","s":"Cell <b>G</b> in the basal layer is a slowly adapting mechanoreceptor. Which statement about it is correct?","o":["It comes from the neural crest and cannot be seen on H&amp;E","It is a dendritic antigen-presenting cell of the mid-to-upper spinous layer","It is the most numerous epidermal cell and drives re-epithelialisation after injury","It occurs at about 1 in every 4 basal cells","It makes melanin inside melanosomes"],"w":["That is the Langerhans cell.","That is the keratinocyte.","That ratio describes melanocytes.","That is the melanocyte."],"e":"G is a Merkel cell: neural crest origin, basal layer, slowly adapting mechanoreceptor for touch linked to sensory nerve endings, more numerous in digits and lips, not visible on H&amp;E.","k":"Merkel = touch, neural crest.","img":{"src":"16/o_epidermis","cap":"Layers of the epidermis (thick skin). Image: OpenStax College : J. Gordon Betts, Peter Desaix, Eddie Johnson., CC BY 3.0, Wikimedia Commons; labels removed."},"rn":[["","Why can melanoma occur outside the skin?","Why can melanoma occur outside the skin?","Melanocytes come from neural crest; Found wherever neural crest cells go"],["","Where do Merkel cells originate?","Where do Merkel cells originate?","Neural crest"]]},{"id":16533,"t":"Epidermis","s":"How are the cells of layer <b>E</b> anchored to the basement membrane beneath them?","o":["Hemidesmosomes","Desmosomes","Keratohyaline granules","Type I collagen fibres","Arrector pili muscle fibres"],"w":["Desmosomes join keratinocytes to each other.","Keratohyaline granules are in the granular layer.","The basement membrane is type IV collagen.","Arrector pili attach to hair follicles."],"e":"Basal keratinocytes are anchored by hemidesmosomes; keratinocytes are joined to each other by desmosomes. The basement membrane is type IV collagen and resists shearing.","k":"Hemi = to the membrane.","img":{"src":"16/o_epidermis","cap":"Layers of the epidermis (thick skin). Image: OpenStax College : J. Gordon Betts, Peter Desaix, Eddie Johnson., CC BY 3.0, Wikimedia Commons; labels removed."},"rn":[["","Desmosomes vs hemidesmosomes: function","Desmosomes vs hemidesmosomes: function","Desmosomes: cell to cell; Hemidesmosomes: cell to basement membrane"],["","How do you remember hemidesmosomes?","How do you remember hemidesmosomes?","Hemi = half, anchors to the base; MNEMONIC"]]},{"id":16534,"t":"Skin Cancer","s":"Each lesion on the left is a melanoma beside a benign mole. Which ABCDE feature does lesion <b>3</b> mainly show?","o":["Colour","Asymmetry","Border","Diameter &gt;6 mm","Evolving"],"w":["Lesion 1 shows asymmetry.","Lesion 2 shows an irregular border.","Lesion 4 shows diameter.","Evolution needs change over time, not one photo."],"e":"ABCDE: Asymmetry, Border, Colour, Diameter &gt;6 mm, Evolving (changing). Lesion 3 has several colours (black, blue-grey, brown).","k":"ABCDE.","img":{"src":"16/o_abcd","cap":"Melanomas (left) beside benign moles (right). Image: Unknown creator; six images merged by User:Stevenfruitsmaak, Public domain, Wikimedia Commons."},"rn":[["","Image card: What type of melanoma is this, and what is ABCDE?","What type of melanoma is this, and what is ABCDE?","Superficial spreading melanoma; Asymmetry, Border, Colour, Diameter >6 mm, Evolving"],["","In ABCDE, what do the letters look for in a mole?","In ABCDE, what do the letters look for in a mole?","Asymmetrical shape; Irregular border; Variable or darker colour; Diameter over 6 mm; Evolving (changing)"]]},{"id":16535,"t":"Skin Cancer","s":"Lesion <b>2</b> has a ragged, notched edge. Which word of the ABCDE checklist does it show?","a":["border","b","irregular border","border irregularity"],"e":"Lesion 2 shows an irregular Border. The full checklist: Asymmetry, Border, Colour, Diameter &gt;6 mm, Evolving.","k":"B = border.","img":{"src":"16/o_abcd","cap":"Melanomas (left) beside benign moles (right). Image: Unknown creator; six images merged by User:Stevenfruitsmaak, Public domain, Wikimedia Commons."},"rn":[["","Image card: What type of melanoma is this, and what is ABCDE?","What type of melanoma is this, and what is ABCDE?","Superficial spreading melanoma; Asymmetry, Border, Colour, Diameter >6 mm, Evolving"],["","In ABCDE, what do the letters look for in a mole?","In ABCDE, what do the letters look for in a mole?","Asymmetrical shape; Irregular border; Variable or darker colour; Diameter over 6 mm; Evolving (changing)"]]},{"id":16536,"t":"Skin Cancer","s":"Lesion <b>4</b> is large. Which ABCDE criterion has a numerical threshold, and what is it?","o":["Diameter greater than 6 mm","Diameter greater than 4 mm","Diameter greater than 1 mm","Border with more than 6 notches","Evolving over more than 6 months"],"w":["4 mm is the Breslow depth for high risk, not diameter.","1 mm is the Breslow cut-off for excellent prognosis.","Border has no number.","Evolving means changing; no set time."],"e":"D = Diameter &gt;6 mm. Do not mix it up with Breslow thickness (depth): &lt;1 mm excellent, &gt;4 mm poor.","k":"D > 6 mm.","img":{"src":"16/o_abcd","cap":"Melanomas (left) beside benign moles (right). Image: Unknown creator; six images merged by User:Stevenfruitsmaak, Public domain, Wikimedia Commons."},"rn":[["","Image card: What type of melanoma is this, and what is ABCDE?","What type of melanoma is this, and what is ABCDE?","Superficial spreading melanoma; Asymmetry, Border, Colour, Diameter >6 mm, Evolving"],["","In ABCDE, what do the letters look for in a mole?","In ABCDE, what do the letters look for in a mole?","Asymmetrical shape; Irregular border; Variable or darker colour; Diameter over 6 mm; Evolving (changing)"]]},{"id":16537,"t":"Skin Cancer","s":"This raised black lesion on a 58-year-old’s back is excised. It measures 5 mm from the granular cell layer to its deepest point. What is the outlook?","o":["High risk of metastasis; poor prognosis","Small risk of metastasis; excellent prognosis","Benign: this type of melanoma does not spread","Locally destructive but rarely metastasises","Prognosis depends only on lesion diameter"],"w":["That applies to Breslow &lt;1 mm.","There is no benign melanoma; all types are malignant.","That describes basal cell carcinoma.","Prognosis is based on Breslow thickness (depth)."],"e":"This is a nodular melanoma. Prognosis is based on Breslow thickness: &gt;4 mm means high metastatic risk and poor prognosis. Melanoma has a propensity to metastasise and is generally worse than BCC/SCC.","k":"Breslow >4 mm = poor.","img":{"src":"16/o_nodular","cap":"Pigmented skin lesion. Image: DermNetNZ, CC BY 4.0, Wikimedia Commons."},"rn":[["","Breslow >4 mm: prognosis?","Breslow >4 mm: prognosis?","High risk of metastasis; Poor prognosis"]]},{"id":16538,"t":"Skin Cancer","s":"This irregular pigmented lesion on the sole of a patient with darker skin is a melanoma. Name the type.","a":["acral lentiginous melanoma","acral lentiginous","acral melanoma","alm"],"e":"Acral lentiginous melanoma. Fairer skin carries more risk overall, but acral sites are at risk in higher skin phototypes. Other types: superficial spreading, nodular, subungual, lentigo maligna; all are malignant.","k":"Sole/palm = acral lentiginous.","img":{"src":"16/o_acral","cap":"Pigmented lesion of the sole. Image: Xavier-Júnior et al., CC BY 4.0, Wikimedia Commons."},"rn":[["","Image card: What type of melanoma is this? Where is it?","What type of melanoma is this? Where is it?","Acral lentiginous melanoma; Palms and soles"],["","What are the 5 types of melanoma?","What are the 5 types of melanoma?","Superficial spreading; Nodular; Acral lentiginous; Subungual; Lentigo maligna"]]},{"id":16539,"t":"Skin Cancer","s":"A 70-year-old has a slowly enlarging lesion on the nose. Biopsy shows these islands of “blue” cells in the dermis, attached to the overlying epithelium. Which behaviour is expected?","o":["Local infiltration and recurrence, but rarely spreads to other organs","Frequent spread to lymph nodes, lung, liver and brain","Arises from melanocytes, so prognosis is set by Breslow thickness in mm","Benign growth that never recurs after excision","A secondary deposit (metastasis) from a lung cancer"],"w":["That is squamous cell carcinoma.","That is melanoma.","BCC is malignant and tends to recur.","The cells come from basal cells of the epidermis, a primary tumour."],"e":"Basal cell carcinoma: most common skin cancer in the Western world, from basal cells, often on the head and neck, caused by UV. It infiltrates locally (rodent ulcer, locally destructive), tends to be multiple and recur, but rarely spreads to other organs.","k":"BCC: local, rarely metastasises.","img":{"src":"16/o_bcchist","cap":"Skin biopsy, H&amp;E. Image: Ed Uthman, MD, CC BY-SA 2.0, Wikimedia Commons."},"rn":[["","What are the 7 features of BCC?","What are the 7 features of BCC?","Most common skin cancer; Head and neck; Infiltrates locally, rarely spreads; Tends to be multiple; Tends to recur; Can be disfiguring on the face; Caused by UV"]]},{"id":16540,"t":"Skin Cancer","s":"A 74-year-old transplant recipient on immunosuppressants has this crusted, ulcerated tumour of sun-exposed skin, arising from squamous cells. Why is its prognosis worse than basal cell carcinoma?","o":["It can metastasise to lymph nodes, lung, liver and brain","It is more common than basal cell carcinoma","It arises from melanocytes of the basal layer","It is always multiple and recurs after excision","It is not related to UV radiation exposure"],"w":["BCC is the most common; SCC is second.","Melanoma arises from melanocytes.","Multiplicity and recurrence describe BCC.","UV is the first listed risk factor."],"e":"Squamous cell carcinoma: second most common skin cancer, on sun-exposed head and neck; unlike BCC it can metastasise (lymph nodes, lung, liver, brain), so prognosis is worse. Risks: UV, previous skin cancer, fair skin, immunosuppression, xeroderma pigmentosum, smoking, arsenic, ageing.","k":"SCC metastasises; BCC rarely.","img":{"src":"16/o_scc","cap":"Skin tumour. Image: Original from National Cancer Institute, USA, CC0, Wikimedia Commons."},"rn":[["","Where can SCC metastasise?","Where can SCC metastasise?","Lymph nodes; Lung; Liver; Brain"]]},{"id":16541,"t":"Skin Cancer","s":"Name this tumour: the second most common skin cancer, linked to immunosuppression, xeroderma pigmentosum and arsenic.","a":["squamous cell carcinoma","scc","cutaneous squamous cell carcinoma","squamous carcinoma"],"e":"Squamous cell carcinoma (from squamous cells of the epidermis).","k":"SCC.","img":{"src":"16/o_scc","cap":"Skin tumour. Image: Original from National Cancer Institute, USA, CC0, Wikimedia Commons."},"rn":[["","Image card: What skin cancer is this?","What skin cancer is this?","Squamous cell carcinoma"],["","SCC","SCC","Squamous cell carcinoma"],["","What are the 3 main types of primary skin cancer?","What are the 3 main types of primary skin cancer?","Squamous cell carcinoma; Basal cell carcinoma; Melanoma"]]},{"id":16542,"t":"Pathology of the Dermis","s":"Months after minor surgery this scar has grown well beyond the margin of the original wound as firm, smooth nodules. What is it?","o":["Keloid scar","Hypertrophic scar confined to the original wound margin","Haemangioma of the dermis","Nodular melanoma","Seborrhoeic keratosis"],"w":["Hypertrophic scars do not extend beyond the wound margin.","A haemangioma is a vascular lesion, not a scar.","This followed a wound and is scar tissue.","Seborrhoeic keratosis is an epidermal lesion."],"e":"Keloid scar: scar tissue extending beyond the margin of the original injury as firm, smooth nodules, with thick, haphazardly arranged collagen bundles. A hypertrophic scar stays within the margin.","k":"Keloid goes beyond the margin.","img":{"src":"16/o_keloid","cap":"Scar on the trunk. Image: Htirgan, CC BY-SA 3.0, Wikimedia Commons."},"rn":[["","Keloid Scar","Keloid Scar","Scar tissue extending beyond the margin of the original injury; Firm, smooth nodules"]]},{"id":16543,"t":"Pathology of the Epidermis","s":"This man has many raised, red, scaly lesions, each more than 1 cm across and forming a plateau-like elevation. Which term describes each lesion, and which layer is mainly affected in this condition?","o":["Plaque; epidermis","Nodule; dermis","Papule; epidermis","Plaque; subcutis","Patch; dermis"],"w":["A nodule is raised and solid but not plateau-like; psoriasis is epidermal.","A papule is &lt;1 cm.","Psoriasis is an epidermal condition.","A patch is flat, not raised."],"e":"Plaque: a raised plateau-like lesion &gt;1 cm. Psoriasis is one of the epidermal conditions. Papule &lt;1 cm; nodule &gt;1 cm solid; macule/patch are flat.","k":"Plaque = raised plateau >1 cm.","img":{"src":"16/o_psoriasis","cap":"Skin lesions on the back and arms. Image: Marnanel, CC BY-SA 3.0, Wikimedia Commons."},"rn":[["","Plaque","Plaque","Raised plateau-like lesion; >1 cm"]]},{"id":16001,"t":"Introduction","s":"A patient with gradually declining renal function complains of severe, untreatable generalised itching. Which link does this illustrate?","o":["Intractable pruritus in chronic renal failure","Intractable pruritus in chronic coeliac disease, from gluten sensitivity","Malar rash in lupus","Splinter haemorrhages in endocarditis","Dermatitis herpetiformis in renal failure"],"e":"The skin is a <b>window into systemic illness</b>. Cutaneous manifestations listed: malar/butterfly rash → <b>lupus</b>; generalised intractable <b>pruritus</b> → <b>chronic renal failure</b>; splinter haemorrhages → <b>bacterial endocarditis/vasculitis</b>; half and half nails; dermatitis herpetiformis → <b>coeliac disease</b>. Itching is non-specific but may indicate underlying disease.","w":["Coeliac links to dermatitis herpetiformis.","Lupus.","Endocarditis.","Coeliac."],"k":"Pruritus ↔ chronic renal failure.","rn":[["","Intractable pruritus.","Intractable pruritus.","chronic renal failure"],["","What are the 5 cutaneous signs of systemic illness listed?","What are the 5 cutaneous signs of systemic illness listed?","Malar/butterfly rash — lupus; Intractable pruritus. — chronic renal failure; Splinter haemorrhages — endocarditis/vasculitis; Half and half nails"]]},{"id":16002,"t":"Introduction","s":"Which pairing of skin finding and systemic disease is correct?","o":["Dermatitis herpetiformis – coeliac disease","Dermatitis herpetiformis – chronic renal failure and itching","Malar rash – chronic renal failure","Splinter haemorrhages – lupus","Pruritus – bacterial endocarditis"],"e":"<b>Dermatitis herpetiformis → coeliac disease</b>; malar rash → lupus; pruritus → chronic renal failure; splinter haemorrhages → endocarditis/vasculitis. Look carefully at the skin, especially the <b>nails</b>, in examination.","w":["Renal failure gives pruritus.","Lupus.","Endocarditis/vasculitis.","Renal failure."],"k":"Dermatitis herpetiformis → coeliac.","rn":[["","Dermatitis herpetiformis is a sign of which disease?","Dermatitis herpetiformis is a sign of which disease?","Coeliac disease (gluten sensitivity)"]]},{"id":16003,"t":"Introduction","s":"Which statement about the skin is correct?","o":["The largest organ, about 1.5–2 m² in adults","The largest organ, about 10–12 m² in most adults","The largest organ, about 0.5 m² in adults","The second-largest organ after the liver","A single-layered organ, about 1.5–2 m²"],"e":"The skin is the <b>largest organ</b>; in most adults it covers about <b>1.5–2 m²</b>. Diagnosis of skin disorders, as in all pathology, can use the <b>surgical sieve</b> or a <b>structure based approach</b>.","w":["1.5–2 m².","1.5–2 m².","Largest.","Layers."],"k":"Largest organ, 1.5–2 m².","rn":[["","What is the largest organ in the body?","What is the largest organ in the body?","The skin"]]},{"id":16004,"t":"Structure of the Skin","f":"sk13","s":"On a low-power H&E section of skin, you move from a thin purple band to a broad pink zone and then to white “bubbles”. What are these in order?","o":["Epidermis, dermis, subcutis (fat)","Dermis, epidermis, subcutis","Subcutis, dermis, epidermis","Epidermis, subcutis, dermis","Basement membrane, epidermis, dermis"],"e":"Three layers: <b>epidermis</b> (keratinising stratified epithelium – the icing, purple), <b>dermis</b> (collagenous tissue – the sponge, pink, with follicles and glands), <b>subcutis</b> (fat – white bubbles). The lecturer stressed using <b>colour gradation</b>: when colours change you move into a different zone.","w":["Epidermis is on top.","Reversed.","Fat is deepest.","The BM is a thin line, not a zone."],"k":"Purple → pink → white bubbles.","rn":[["","Image card: What are the 3 layers of skin seen here, top to bottom? How can you tell?","What are the 3 layers of skin seen here, top to bottom? How can you tell?","Epidermis: purple; Dermis: pink; Sub cutis: white bubbly fat"]]},{"id":16005,"t":"Structure of the Skin","s":"A skin biopsy shows a very thick dermis. Which site is most likely?","o":["The trunk/back, unlike the thin-skinned eyelid","The eyelid, which has the thickest dermis of all sites","The palm, which lacks a dermis entirely","The lip, which lacks an epidermis","Any site, as thickness is uniform"],"e":"Thickness varies with site: an <b>eyelid</b> biopsy has thin skin with little dermis; the <b>back</b> has thick dermis. The example image was probably from the <b>trunk</b> because of its thick dermis. The epidermis is <b>thinner</b> than the dermis.","w":["Eyelid is thin.","Not.","Not.","Varies."],"k":"Dermal thickness varies by site.","rn":[["","Does skin thickness vary by site?","Does skin thickness vary by site?","Yes; e.g. eyelid thin, back thick"]]},{"id":16006,"t":"Epidermis","s":"“Keratinising stratified squamous epithelium.” Which interpretation of each word is correct?","o":["Matures to keratin; many layers; flat cells; cohesive sheets","Alive at the surface; many layers; flat cells; cohesive sheets","Matures to keratin; one layer; flat cells; cohesive sheets","Matures to keratin; many layers; columnar cells; cohesive sheets","Matures to keratin; many layers; flat cells; loose connective tissue"],"e":"<b>Keratinising</b>: cells mature towards the surface to produce keratin. <b>Stratified</b>: lots of layers. <b>Squamous</b>: flat, unlike cuboidal/columnar GI cells. <b>Epithelium</b>: sheets of closely packed <b>cohesive</b> cells with tight junctions. Maturation cycle ≈ 20–40 days (notes); about 28 days (recording).","w":["Dead at surface.","Many layers.","Flat.","Cohesive cells."],"k":"Unpacking the epidermis definition.","rn":[["","What does \"epithelium\" mean?","What does \"epithelium\" mean?","Sheets of closely packed cohesive cells"]]},{"id":16007,"t":"Epidermis","s":"What are the natural openings in the continuous epidermal sheet?","o":["Hair follicles and sweat ducts (acrosyringium)","Gaps where the basement membrane is absent","Holes made by Langerhans cells","Spaces formed by spongiosis","Openings for blood vessels to the surface"],"e":"The epidermis forms a <b>continuous layer</b> with <b>natural openings</b> for <b>hair follicles</b> and <b>sweat ducts</b> (<b>acrosyringium</b>). Sebaceous glands release contents into follicles.","w":["BM continuous.","Not.","Pathological.","Not."],"k":"Acrosyringium = sweat duct opening.","rn":[["","What are the natural openings in the epidermis?","What are the natural openings in the epidermis?","Hair follicles; Sweat ducts (acrosyringium)"]]},{"id":16008,"t":"Epidermis","f":"sk18","s":"Which ordering of the epidermal layers from the surface to the deep is correct for non-acral skin?","o":["Corneum → granulosum → spinosum → basale","Corneum → spinosum → granulosum → basale","Basale → spinosum → granulosum → corneum","Corneum → lucidum → granulosum → spinosum","Granulosum → corneum → spinosum → basale"],"e":"Surface → deep: <b>corneum</b> (horn cell), <b>granulosum</b> (granular cell), <b>spinosum</b> (prickle cell), <b>basale</b> (basal cell). Acral skin adds <b>lucidum</b> under the corneum (five layers). In exams, state whether you describe surface→deep or the reverse.","w":["Granulosum is second.","Deep → surface.","Lucidum only acral.","Corneum on top."],"k":"Corneum, (lucidum), granulosum, spinosum, basale.","rn":[["","What are the 4 layers of the epidermis (surface to deep)?","What are the 4 layers of the epidermis (surface to deep)?","Stratum corneum (horn cell); Stratum granulosum (granular cell); Stratum spinosum (prickle cell); Stratum basale (basal cell)"],["","Image card: Name the 4 layers marked by the arrows, top to bottom.","Name the 4 layers marked by the arrows, top to bottom.","Horn cell layer (stratum corneum); Granular cell layer (stratum granulosum); Prickle cell layer (stratum spinosum); Basal cell layer (stratum basale)"]]},{"id":16009,"t":"Epidermis","s":"A biopsy from the sole of the foot has an extra clear layer of dead keratinocytes. Name it.","e":"Stratum lucidum is found in acral skin (palms, soles), resisting friction.","k":"Stratum lucidum.","a":["stratum lucidum","lucidum"],"rn":[["","What is the stratum lucidum?","What is the stratum lucidum?","Clear layer of dead keratinocytes"],["","Why do palms and soles have a stratum lucidum?","Why do palms and soles have a stratum lucidum?","Exposed to a lot of friction; Need extra protection"],["","What is the 5th layer of the epidermis?","What is the 5th layer of the epidermis?","Stratum lucidum"]]},{"id":16010,"t":"Epidermis","s":"Which layer is described as the proliferative compartment, single-layered, basophilic and anchored by hemidesmosomes?","o":["Stratum basale","Stratum spinosum","Stratum granulosum","Stratum corneum","Stratum lucidum"],"e":"<b>Stratum basale</b>: single layer, columnar/cuboidal shape, <b>basophilic (blue)</b>, variable melanin pigmentation, attached to the BM by <b>hemidesmosomes</b>; the <b>proliferative compartment</b> from which new keratinocytes grow up. Basal cells are plumper with bigger nuclei.","w":["Pink bulk, desmosomes.","Granules, waterproofing.","Anucleate.","Acral clear layer."],"k":"Basale: blue, proliferative, hemidesmosomes.","rn":[["","What is the function of the stratum basale?","What is the function of the stratum basale?","Proliferative compartment; New keratinocytes grow to the surface"],["","What shape are stratum basale cells?","What shape are stratum basale cells?","Columnar / cuboidal"],["","How many cells thick is the stratum basale?","How many cells thick is the stratum basale?","A single layer"]]},{"id":16011,"t":"Epidermis","s":"Which statement about the stratum spinosum is correct?","o":["5–10 cells thick, pink polyhedral cells with desmosome prickles","1–3 cells thick, flattened cells with keratohyaline granules","A single layer of blue cuboidal cells","Anucleate cells with basketweave keratin","5–10 cells thick, found only in palms and soles"],"e":"<b>Stratum spinosum (prickle cell layer)</b>: <b>5–10 cells thick</b>, main bulk; <b>polyhedral</b> keratinocytes; <b>pink (eosinophilic)</b>; flatter nearer the surface. Named after the <b>spinous processes (“prickles”)</b> between cells, mainly <b>desmosomes</b>.","w":["Granulosum.","Basale.","Corneum.","All sites."],"k":"Spinosum: bulk, pink, desmosome prickles.","rn":[["","How thick is the stratum spinosum?","How thick is the stratum spinosum?","5 to 10 cells thick; Main bulk of the epidermis"]]},{"id":16012,"t":"Epidermis","s":"What is the function of the keratohyaline granules of the stratum granulosum?","o":["They contain lipid and act as waterproofing","They contain melanin and give colour","They contain keratin and anchor cells","They contain antigen for presentation","They contain sweat for cooling"],"e":"<b>Stratum granulosum</b>: <b>1–3 layers</b> of flattened (almost dead) cells containing basophilic <b>keratohyaline granules</b>, which contain <b>lipid</b> and act as <b>waterproofing</b>/protection.","w":["Melanosomes.","Not.","Langerhans.","Not."],"k":"Granulosum: lipid waterproofing.","rn":[["","What do keratohyaline granules contain?","What do keratohyaline granules contain?","Lipid"]]},{"id":16013,"t":"Epidermis","s":"Why is having dead, anucleate cells in the stratum corneum described as “beautifully suited” to function?","o":["The most exposed cells are dead, so cannot be damaged","The most exposed cells divide fastest to replace those lost by friction","The most exposed cells make melanin for protection","The most exposed cells present antigen to lymph nodes","The most exposed cells absorb vitamin D"],"e":"<b>Stratum corneum</b>: polyhedral eosinophilic keratinocytes packed with keratin filaments, <b>no nucleus</b>, basketweave keratin. From the recording: the most exposed cells are <b>dead</b>, so they won't react or be damaged like metabolically active cells. Pink with no purple = no nuclei.","w":["Corneum cells are dead.","Melanocytes.","Langerhans.","Not."],"k":"Dead corneum protects.","rn":[["","Why are the surface cells dead?","Why are the surface cells dead?","Dead cells won't react to friction, sunlight, chemicals; Better protection"],["","Which epidermal layer is easiest to find?","Which epidermal layer is easiest to find?","Stratum corneum; Basket of dead cells on the surface"]]},{"id":16014,"t":"Epidermis","s":"Which labelling of layers A–D is correct?","o":["A corneum; B granulosum; C spinosum; D basale","A corneum; B spinosum; C granulosum; D basale","A granulosum; B corneum; C spinosum; D basale","A corneum; B granulosum; C basale; D spinosum","A lucidum; B granulosum; C spinosum; D basale"],"e":"Surface to deep: stratum corneum (A, anucleate keratin), granulosum (B, keratohyaline granules), spinosum (C, 5–10 cells of pink polyhedral cells with desmosome “prickles”), basale (D, single proliferative layer anchored by hemidesmosomes).","w":["The thin dark granular band (B) lies just under the corneum.","The anucleate keratin (A) is on top.","D, the single row at the base, is the basale.","The lucidum is only in acral skin (palms and soles)."],"k":"Corneum, granulosum, spinosum, basale.","img":{"src":"16/layers","cap":"Epidermis, H&amp;E. Labels removed."},"rn":[["","What are the 4 layers of the epidermis (surface to deep)?","What are the 4 layers of the epidermis (surface to deep)?","Stratum corneum (horn cell); Stratum granulosum (granular cell); Stratum spinosum (prickle cell); Stratum basale (basal cell)"],["","Image card: Name the 4 layers marked by the arrows, top to bottom.","Name the 4 layers marked by the arrows, top to bottom.","Horn cell layer (stratum corneum); Granular cell layer (stratum granulosum); Prickle cell layer (stratum spinosum); Basal cell layer (stratum basale)"]]},{"id":16015,"t":"Epidermis","s":"An itchy rash biopsy shows fluid between keratinocytes, making the prickles obvious. Name this change.","e":"Spongiosis (fluid between keratinocytes) is typical of eczema.","k":"Spongiosis.","a":["spongiosis"],"rn":[["","What is Spongiosis?","What is Spongiosis?","Fluid seeps between keratinocytes in the stratum spinosum; Prickles become more obvious"]]},{"id":16016,"t":"Epidermis","s":"Name the layer labelled <b>D</b>: a single row of basophilic cells anchored to the basement membrane by hemidesmosomes.","e":"D is the stratum basale, the proliferative compartment; it also contains melanocytes and Merkel cells.","k":"D = stratum basale.","img":{"src":"16/layers","cap":"Epidermis, H&amp;E. Labels removed."},"a":["stratum basale","basale","basal layer","basal cell layer","stratum germinativum"],"rn":[["","How many cells thick is the stratum basale?","How many cells thick is the stratum basale?","A single layer"],["","What is the function of the stratum basale?","What is the function of the stratum basale?","Proliferative compartment; New keratinocytes grow to the surface"],["","What are the 4 layers of the epidermis (surface to deep)?","What are the 4 layers of the epidermis (surface to deep)?","Stratum corneum (horn cell); Stratum granulosum (granular cell); Stratum spinosum (prickle cell); Stratum basale (basal cell)"]]},{"id":16017,"t":"Epidermis","s":"Which pairing of junction and role is correct?","o":["Desmosomes: cell to cell; hemidesmosomes: basal cells to BM","Hemidesmosomes: cell to cell; desmosomes: basal cells to BM","Desmosomes: cell to dermis; hemidesmosomes: cell to cell","Both join dermal fibroblasts together","Neither is found in the epidermis"],"e":"Keratinocytes are held together by <b>desmosomes</b> and anchored to the BM by <b>hemidesmosomes</b>. This matters in <b>autoimmune blistering diseases</b>: antibodies to desmosomes split cells within the epidermis; to hemidesmosomes, at its base.","w":["Swapped.","Not.","Epidermis.","They are."],"k":"Desmosome cell–cell; hemidesmosome cell–BM.","rn":[["","Desmosomes vs hemidesmosomes: function","Desmosomes vs hemidesmosomes: function","Desmosomes: cell to cell; Hemidesmosomes: cell to basement membrane"]]},{"id":16018,"t":"Epidermis","s":"A blister splits below the whole epidermis. Name the junctions targeted by autoantibody.","e":"Subepidermal blister → hemidesmosomes (basal cells to basement membrane); intraepidermal → desmosomes.","k":"Hemidesmosomes.","a":["hemidesmosomes","hemidesmosome"],"rn":[["","Desmosomes vs hemidesmosomes: function","Desmosomes vs hemidesmosomes: function","Desmosomes: cell to cell; Hemidesmosomes: cell to basement membrane"],["","What can a subepidermal blister suggest?","What can a subepidermal blister suggest?","Antibodies to hemidesmosomes"]]},{"id":16019,"t":"Introduction","s":"Blistering/bullous lesions and induration point to which skin layers respectively?","o":["Blistering → epidermis; induration → dermis","Blistering → dermis; induration → epidermis","Blistering → subcutis; induration → dermis","Both → epidermis","Both → dermis"],"e":"Key take-home: understanding skin structure is essential to understand function in health and disease – e.g. <b>induration → dermis</b>; <b>blistering/bullous lesions → epidermis</b>.","w":["Swapped.","Not.","Induration dermis.","Blistering epidermis."],"k":"Induration dermis; blistering epidermis.","rn":[["","Induration is a problem of which skin layer?","Induration is a problem of which skin layer?","Dermis"],["","Blistering (bullous) lesions are a problem of which skin layer?","Blistering (bullous) lesions are a problem of which skin layer?","Epidermis"],["","What should you ask yourself when you see a blister?","What should you ask yourself when you see a blister?","Where is it forming?; Between epidermis and dermis, or between epidermal layers?"]]},{"id":16020,"t":"Epidermis","s":"Why is it “not totally out of place” to find a melanoma in the eye or oesophagus?","o":["Melanocytes come from the neural crest, which reaches there","Melanocytes are epithelial cells present in every organ of the body","Melanoma in those sites is always a metastasis","The eye and oesophagus contain keratinocytes","UV light reaches internal organs"],"e":"Melanocytes are <b>dendritic cells from the neural crest</b>, <b>not epithelial</b>; they migrate to the skin during development. From the recording: <b>anywhere neural crest cells go, you can have a melanoma</b>. Melanoma occurs in the <b>eye (ocular)</b>, <b>vulva, anus, oesophagus</b>.","w":["Neural crest, not epithelial.","Not always.","Not the reason.","Not."],"k":"Neural crest origin → melanoma at many sites.","rn":[["","Why can melanoma occur outside the skin?","Why can melanoma occur outside the skin?","Melanocytes come from neural crest; Found wherever neural crest cells go"]]},{"id":16021,"t":"Epidermis","s":"A baby is born with a birthmark formed by a collection of melanocytes. What is the explanation?","o":["Abnormal migration and clustering of melanocytes – congenital naevus","Abnormal migration and clustering of keratinocytes – congenital naevus","Overproduction of keratin – seborrhoeic keratosis","Infection of Langerhans cells at birth","Breach of the basement membrane at birth"],"e":"During development melanocytes <b>migrate to the skin</b>; <b>abnormal migration and concentration</b> at a single site leads to a <b>congenital naevus</b> (birth mark, present at birth). A benign collection = naevus (mole).","w":["Melanocytes.","Not.","Not.","Not."],"k":"Congenital naevus = abnormal migration.","rn":[["","What causes a congenital naevus (birth mark)?","What causes a congenital naevus (birth mark)?","Abnormal migration and concentration of melanocytes at one site"]]},{"id":16022,"t":"Epidermis","f":"sk24","s":"How do melanocytes appear on H&E?","o":["Basal layer, smaller cells with oval nuclei and a halo; ~1 per 4 basal cells","Spinous layer, large pink cells with round nuclei; ~1 per 4 basal cells","Basal layer, larger cells with round nuclei; ~1 per 40 basal cells","Dermis, spindle cells with no halo","Corneum, anucleate cells"],"e":"Melanocytes: located in the <b>basal layer</b>; about <b>1:4</b> basal cells; <b>smaller</b> than basal cells; <b>elongated oval nuclei</b> with a <b>clear space (halo)</b>; produce <b>melanin</b> in <b>melanosomes</b>, transferred to neighbouring cells. The ratio varies by site; melanocytes pointed to elsewhere would be worrying.","w":["Basal, small, halo.","Smaller; 1:4.","Not.","Not."],"k":"Melanocyte: basal, clear halo, 1:4.","rn":[["","Image card: What cells do the yellow arrows point to? How can you tell?","What cells do the yellow arrows point to? How can you tell?","Melanocytes; In the basal layer; Oval nuclei with a clear halo"]]},{"id":16023,"t":"Epidermis","s":"Two people have very different skin colour. What mainly explains this?","o":["Melanin type, melanosome number, spread and breakdown","The number of melanocytes in the basal layer","Melanin type and the number of melanocytes in the basal layer","The thickness of the stratum corneum","The number of Langerhans cells"],"e":"Skin colour depends on the <b>type of melanin</b>, <b>number of melanosomes</b>, their <b>distribution</b> and how quickly they are <b>degraded</b>. An increased <b>number</b> of melanocytes is <b>pathological</b>.","w":["Increased number is pathological.","Not melanocyte number.","Not.","Not."],"k":"Colour = melanin/melanosomes, not melanocyte number.","rn":[["","What determines skin colour?","What determines skin colour?","Type of melanin; Number of melanosomes; Distribution and degradation"]]},{"id":16024,"t":"Epidermis","s":"What is the main protective function of melanin?","o":["Photoprotection: absorbing UV to reduce DNA damage","Photoprotection: reflecting all UV so none enters skin","Waterproofing of the stratum corneum","Antigen presentation to lymph nodes","Thermoregulation by absorbing heat"],"e":"Melanin absorbs harmful UV and protects the skin: <b>photoprotection</b>, reducing <b>UV-mediated DNA damage</b>. Darker skin does <b>not</b> make anyone immune to skin cancer; it probably means a <b>lower incidence</b> of UV-related cancers.","w":["Absorbs and disperses.","Granules.","Langerhans.","Not."],"k":"Melanin = photoprotection.","rn":[["","What does melanin do?","What does melanin do?","Absorbs harmful UV rays; Reduces UV DNA damage; Photoprotection"]]},{"id":16025,"t":"Epidermis","s":"Which statement about Fitzpatrick skin types is correct?","o":["Six types by UV burning and tanning; 1–2 burn easily","Six types by UV burning and tanning; 5–6 burn easily","Three types by UV burning and tanning; 1 burns easily","Six types by melanocyte number","A classification of ethnicity"],"e":"<b>Fitzpatrick</b>: six types based on response to UV – “how the skin burns, how it tans”. <b>Types 1–2</b> burn easily, tan poorly; <b>5–6</b> less prone to sunburn with lower incidence of UV-related cancers. It is <b>not a function of race</b>.","w":["5–6 less prone.","Six.","UV response.","Not."],"k":"Fitzpatrick: burn/tan response.","rn":[["","Which Fitzpatrick types burn easily and tan poorly?","Which Fitzpatrick types burn easily and tan poorly?","Types 1 and 2"],["","What is Fitzpatrick Skin Type?","What is Fitzpatrick Skin Type?","Classifies skin into 6 types; By response to UV exposure (burn vs tan)"]]},{"id":16026,"t":"Epidermis","s":"Name the antigen-presenting cells of the mid–upper spinous layer that carry antigen to lymph nodes.","e":"Langerhans cells are APCs in the spinous layer.","k":"Langerhans cells.","a":["langerhans cells","langerhans cell","langerhans"],"rn":[["","What do Langerhans cells do?","What do Langerhans cells do?","Sample antigens; Present them to immune cells in lymph nodes"],["","Where are Langerhans cells found?","Where are Langerhans cells found?","Mid to upper spinous layer and dermis; All squamous epithelium, other epithelium; Lymphoid organs"],["","What is Langerhans Cells?","What is Langerhans Cells?","Dendritic antigen presenting cells; Immune surveillance of the skin"]]},{"id":16027,"t":"Epidermis","s":"Which statement about Merkel cells is correct?","o":["Neural crest, basal layer, touch receptors, not seen on H&E","Neural crest, basal layer, antigen-presenting, not seen on H&E","Epithelial, corneum, touch receptors, easily seen on H&E","Neural crest, spinous layer, melanin producers","Mesodermal, dermis, touch receptors"],"e":"<b>Merkel cells</b>: <b>neural crest</b>; <b>basal layer</b>; <b>slowly adapting mechanoreceptors</b> for tactile sensation (sustained touch), associated with sensory nerve endings; <b>increased in digits, lips</b>; <b>not seen on H&E</b>. Rare Merkel cell carcinoma exists – any skin cell can become a cancer.","w":["Langerhans.","Not.","Melanocytes.","Not."],"k":"Merkel: touch, basal, not on H&E.","rn":[["","Where do Merkel cells originate?","Where do Merkel cells originate?","Neural crest"],["","Where are Merkel cells located?","Where are Merkel cells located?","Basal layer"],["","Why can melanoma occur outside the skin?","Why can melanoma occur outside the skin?","Melanocytes come from neural crest; Found wherever neural crest cells go"]]},{"id":16028,"t":"Epidermis","f":"sk28","s":"An atypical proliferation of squamous cells is seen to breach the basement membrane. What is the significance?","o":["Invasion: in situ has become invasive cancer","Spongiosis: fluid between keratinocytes","Normal maturation of squamous cells","A benign lesion that remains in situ","A sign of acral skin"],"e":"The <b>basement membrane</b> (type IV collagen; resists shearing; structural; controls epithelial–mesenchymal interactions) is the pink line on PAS. If atypical cells <b>breach</b> it, this is <b>invasion</b>: <b>in situ → invasive cancer</b>. Before calling something a carcinoma, it must have invaded (e.g. CIN is precancerous, not yet invaded).","w":["Not.","Not.","Not.","Not."],"k":"BM breach = invasion.","rn":[["","What happens if atypical cells breach the basement membrane?","What happens if atypical cells breach the basement membrane?","Invasion; In situ becomes invasive cancer"]]},{"id":16029,"t":"Epidermis","s":"Which collagen type makes up the basement membrane and which stain highlighted it?","o":["Type IV collagen; PAS","Type I collagen; PAS","Type IV collagen; H&E","Type III collagen; silver","Type II collagen; trichrome"],"e":"BM: <b>type IV collagen</b>, shown as the pink line on <b>PAS</b> stain; hard to see on H&E. Cancer cells can produce proteins that <b>dissolve</b> BM collagen and invade the dermis.","w":["IV.","Hard on H&E.","Reticular.","Cartilage."],"k":"BM: type IV, PAS.","rn":[["","What type of collagen forms the basement membrane?","What type of collagen forms the basement membrane?","Type IV collagen"]]},{"id":16030,"t":"Pathology of the Epidermis","f":"sk31","s":"A patient has well-demarcated raised red plaques with silvery scale on the elbows and knees. What would the histology show?","o":["Acanthosis, thick keratin and dilated vessels near the surface","Acanthosis, thick keratin and blue nests attached to the epidermis","Thin epidermis with no keratin and dilated vessels","Keloid collagen bundles in the dermis","Grouped blood vessels deep in the dermis"],"e":"<b>Psoriasis</b>: well demarcated, raised, <b>erythematous</b> plaques with <b>silvery scale</b>. Histology: <b>thickened epidermis (acanthosis)</b>, <b>lots of keratin</b> on top (plaque and scale); <b>redness</b> from <b>dilated blood vessels</b> high near the keratin. Pathology of <b>rapid turnover</b>: squamous cells migrate up very quickly and are shed.","w":["BCC.","Thick.","Scar.","Haemangioma."],"k":"Psoriasis: acanthosis, keratin, dilated vessels.","rn":[["","Why is psoriasis red?","Why is psoriasis red?","Dilated blood vessels high up near the keratin"]]},{"id":16031,"t":"Pathology of the Epidermis","s":"What is the underlying pathological process of psoriasis described?","o":["Rapid turnover: cells rise quickly and are shed","Slow turnover: cells accumulate without shedding","Basement membrane breach by atypical cells","Proliferation of melanocytes","Fluid seeping between keratinocytes"],"e":"Psoriasis is a pathology of <b>rapid turnover</b>. It is predominantly <b>epidermal</b>, not really involving the dermis.","w":["Rapid.","Invasion.","Not.","Spongiosis."],"k":"Psoriasis = rapid turnover.","rn":[["","What is the basic pathology of psoriasis?","What is the basic pathology of psoriasis?","Rapid turnover; Cells migrate up quickly and are shed"]]},{"id":16032,"t":"Pathology of the Epidermis","f":"sk33","s":"Why are seborrhoeic keratoses clinically important even though they are benign?","o":["They can be hard to tell from melanoma","They can be hard to tell from psoriasis plaques","They often metastasise to lymph nodes","They are premalignant SCC lesions","They breach the basement membrane"],"e":"<b>Seborrhoeic keratosis</b> (“Seb K”, seborrhoeic wart): <b>completely benign</b>, very common, looks like a “<b>stuck on cornflake</b>”, but can be <b>difficult to tell from melanoma</b>. Histology: <b>acanthosis</b> with little <b>keratin-filled nests</b> within the epidermis.","w":["Melanoma.","Benign.","Benign.","Not."],"k":"Seb K: benign, mimics melanoma.","rn":[["","Why are seborrhoeic keratoses important?","Why are seborrhoeic keratoses important?","Can be hard to tell from melanoma"]]},{"id":16033,"t":"Pathology of the Epidermis","f":"sk34","s":"How does the histology of seborrhoeic keratosis differ from psoriasis?","o":["Keratin-filled nests within the epidermis","Keratin only on the surface, as in psoriasis","Blue nests in the dermis attached to the overlying epidermis","No acanthosis, unlike psoriasis","Identical histology to psoriasis"],"e":"Both are predominantly <b>epidermal</b> with <b>acanthosis</b>; Seb K has <b>nests containing keratin within the epidermis</b> rather than only on the surface.","w":["Psoriasis.","BCC.","Both thickened.","Different."],"k":"Seb K: intraepidermal keratin nests.","rn":[["","What does seborrhoeic keratosis show on histology?","What does seborrhoeic keratosis show on histology?","Acanthosis; Nests of keratin within the epidermis"],["","Image card: What condition shows this histology?","What condition shows this histology?","Seborrhoeic keratosis; Acanthosis; Keratin nests within the epidermis"]]},{"id":16034,"t":"Dermis","s":"Which statement about the dermis is correct?","o":["Dense connective tissue supporting the epidermis","Dense connective tissue lying above the epidermis, carrying vessels","Loose fatty tissue beneath the subcutis","A keratinising epithelium with appendages","An acellular layer of collagen only"],"e":"<b>Dermis</b>: <b>dense connective tissue</b> (collagen, elastin, ECM – a hydrated protein gel with glycosaminoglycans, hyaluronan, proteoglycans); <b>supports the epidermis</b>; contains nerves, vessels, lymphatics, mechano-/thermoreceptors, and appendages (pilosebaceous units, sweat glands). Every epithelium has a supporting stroma; in skin it is the dermis.","w":["Beneath.","Subcutis.","Epidermis.","Cells."],"k":"Dermis = supporting dense CT.","rn":[["","What type of tissue is the dermis?","What type of tissue is the dermis?","Dense connective tissue"],["","What does the dermis do?","What does the dermis do?","Supports the epidermis"]]},{"id":16035,"t":"Dermis","s":"Which pairing of dermal cell and category is correct?","o":["Fibroblasts – resident; eosinophils, neutrophils – transient","Fibroblasts – transient; eosinophils, neutrophils – resident","Neutrophils – resident; adipocytes – transient","Eosinophils – resident; fibroblasts – transient","Adipocytes – transient; neutrophils – resident"],"e":"Dermal cells: <b>fibroblasts</b>, <b>histiocytes</b>, vascular smooth muscle, leucocytes (lymphocytes, mast cells, plasma cells), <b>transient</b> cells (<b>eosinophils, neutrophils</b>), adipocytes. <b>Resident</b> cells (mainly fibroblasts) are always there; <b>transient</b> cells move in and out (mainly inflammatory), depending on what the skin is going through.","w":["Swapped.","Not.","Not.","Not."],"k":"Resident vs transient dermal cells.","rn":[["","What 2 transient cells are in the dermis?","What 2 transient cells are in the dermis?","Eosinophils; Neutrophils"],["","What are the 2 groups of dermal cells?","What are the 2 groups of dermal cells?","Resident (always there); Transient (move in and out)"]]},{"id":16036,"t":"Dermis","s":"Acne is centred on the unit that includes the gland labelled <b>D</b>, which drains into the hair follicle. Name gland D.","e":"D is a sebaceous gland: lipid-filled (foamy) sebocytes drain into the follicle. Hair follicle + sebaceous gland(s) + arrector pili = the pilosebaceous unit, the centre of acne.","k":"D = sebaceous gland.","img":{"src":"16/block","cap":"Block diagram of skin. Labels removed."},"a":["sebaceous gland","sebaceous","sebaceous glands","oil gland"],"rn":[["","Where do sebaceous glands drain?","Where do sebaceous glands drain?","Into the hair follicle; Via the sebaceous duct at the isthmus"],["","What are the 3 parts of the pilosebaceous unit?","What are the 3 parts of the pilosebaceous unit?","Hair follicles; Sebaceous glands; Arrector pili muscle"],["","How many sebaceous glands per follicle?","How many sebaceous glands per follicle?","Multiple glands per follicle"]]},{"id":16037,"t":"Dermis","f":"sk41","s":"Which feature distinguishes fibroblasts from histiocytes?","o":["Fibroblasts: spindle, make collagen; histiocytes: bean-shaped, phagocytic","Fibroblasts: bean-shaped, phagocytic; histiocytes: spindle, make collagen","Fibroblasts: from monocytes; histiocytes: from neural crest","Fibroblasts form giant cells; histiocytes make collagen","Both are terminally differentiated spindle cells"],"e":"<b>Fibroblasts</b>: spindle shaped; <b>not terminally differentiated</b>; synthesise <b>ECM and collagen</b>; inflammation, <b>wound healing</b>, angiogenesis; <b>most common cell in CT</b>. <b>Histiocytes</b>: variable size, <b>bean-shaped nuclei</b>, can form <b>giant cells</b>; <b>fully differentiated</b>; migrate from <b>bone marrow as monocytes</b>; <b>antigen presentation</b>, <b>phagocytosis</b>. Histiocyte = macrophage.","w":["Swapped.","Monocytes → histiocytes.","Histiocytes form giant cells.","Fibroblasts are not."],"k":"Fibroblast vs histiocyte.","rn":[["","Fibroblasts","Fibroblasts","Spindle shaped cells; Make extracellular matrix and collagen"],["","Image card: What cells are these? What are they making?","What cells are these? What are they making?","Fibroblasts (spindle shaped); Collagen fibres"]]},{"id":16038,"t":"Dermis","s":"Monocytes leave the blood and enter the dermis. What are they then called?","e":"Monocytes become histiocytes in tissue: bean-shaped, phagocytic, can form giant cells.","k":"Histiocytes.","a":["histiocytes","histiocyte","macrophages","macrophage"],"rn":[["","What can histiocytes form?","What can histiocytes form?","Giant cells"]]},{"id":16039,"t":"Dermis","f":"sk43","s":"What makes up the pilosebaceous unit?","o":["Hair follicle, sebaceous gland(s), arrector pili","Hair follicle, sweat gland, arrector pili","Hair follicle, sebaceous gland(s), myoepithelial cells","Sebaceous gland, sweat gland, sweat duct","Melanocytes, keratinocytes, hair"],"e":"<b>Pilosebaceous unit</b>: <b>hair follicle</b> (pilo), <b>sebaceous gland(s)</b>, <b>arrector pili muscle</b>. Sweat glands (eccrine, apocrine) are the other appendages. <b>Acne</b> centres on the pilosebaceous unit.","w":["Sebaceous.","Arrector pili.","Not.","Not."],"k":"Pilosebaceous = follicle + gland + arrector pili.","rn":[["","What are the 3 parts of the pilosebaceous unit?","What are the 3 parts of the pilosebaceous unit?","Hair follicles; Sebaceous glands; Arrector pili muscle"]]},{"id":16040,"t":"Dermis","f":"sk44","s":"Which statement about hair is correct?","o":["Keratin, no living cells; melanin colour; anagen–catagen–telogen","Keratin with living cells; melanin colour; anagen–catagen–telogen","Keratin, no living cells; keratin colour; anagen–catagen–telogen","Keratin, no living cells; melanin colour; no growth cycle","Collagen, no living cells; melanin colour; anagen–telogen"],"e":"Hair: formed from <b>keratin</b>, <b>no living cells</b>; follicle produces the shaft; colour from <b>type and quantity of melanin</b>; cycle: <b>anagen, catagen, telogen</b>. The follicle is a <b>continuation of the epidermis</b> with living keratinocytes at the base.","w":["No living cells.","Melanin.","Cycle.","Keratin."],"k":"Hair: keratin, melanin, anagen/catagen/telogen.","rn":[["","What are the 3 phases of the hair cycle?","What are the 3 phases of the hair cycle?","Anagen; Catagen; Telogen"]]},{"id":16041,"t":"Dermis","f":"sk46","s":"Why do sebaceous glands look foamy, and where do they drain?","o":["Lipid-filled sebocytes; drain into the follicle","Mucus-filled cells; drain directly onto the skin","Lipid-filled sebocytes that drain directly onto the skin surface","Melanin-filled cells; drain into vessels","Keratin-filled cells; drain into sweat ducts"],"e":"<b>Sebaceous glands</b>: formed from <b>sebocytes</b>; <b>foamy</b> with <b>lipid-based secretions</b>; drain into the <b>hair follicle</b> via the <b>sebaceous duct</b> at the <b>isthmus</b>; multiple glands per follicle.","w":["Lipid.","Into follicles.","Not.","Not."],"k":"Sebaceous: foamy lipid, isthmus.","rn":[["","Where do sebaceous glands drain?","Where do sebaceous glands drain?","Into the hair follicle; Via the sebaceous duct at the isthmus"],["","Image card: What is this structure? How can you tell?","What is this structure? How can you tell?","Sebaceous gland; Foamy sebocytes (lipid secretions)"]]},{"id":16042,"t":"Dermis","f":"sk48","s":"A gland in the axilla has columnar cells that release secretion as little caps fall off. Which gland is it and its function?","o":["Apocrine – body odour","Eccrine – temperature control","Sebaceous – sebum","Eccrine – body odour","Apocrine – temperature control"],"e":"<b>Apocrine</b>: axilla, groin, nipple; <b>columnar</b> cells with “<b>decapitation</b>” secretion (cap falls off); pinker; <b>body odour</b>. <b>Eccrine</b>: all over body; <b>cuboidal</b> cells surrounded by <b>myoepithelial</b> cells; bluer; <b>temperature control</b>; open directly onto the skin.","w":["Eccrine is everywhere, cuboidal.","Foamy sebocytes.","Wrong function.","Wrong function."],"k":"Apocrine: axilla, decapitation, odour.","rn":[["","Eccrine vs apocrine: function","Eccrine vs apocrine: function","Eccrine: temperature control; Apocrine: body odour"],["","What are apocrine glands responsible for?","What are apocrine glands responsible for?","Body odour"]]},{"id":16043,"t":"Dermis","s":"Which sweat gland is most important for thermoregulation, and how does it appear?","o":["Eccrine – blue cuboidal cells with myoepithelium, everywhere","Apocrine – blue cuboidal cells with myoepithelium, everywhere","Eccrine – pink columnar cells, axilla only","Apocrine – pink columnar cells, everywhere","Sebaceous – foamy cells, everywhere"],"e":"<b>Eccrine</b> glands: <b>temperature control</b>, all over the body, cuboidal cells + myoepithelial cells, bluer.","w":["Eccrine.","Not.","Apocrine.","Not."],"k":"Eccrine = thermoregulation.","rn":[["","Image card: What type of sweat gland is this? How can you tell?","What type of sweat gland is this? How can you tell?","Eccrine; Blue cuboidal cells; Myoepithelial cells around"],["","What cells make eccrine glands?","What cells make eccrine glands?","Cuboidal cells; Surrounded by myoepithelial cells"]]},{"id":16044,"t":"Pathology of the Dermis","s":"After surgery, a scar forms firm, smooth nodules extending well beyond the original wound. Name it.","e":"Keloid: extends beyond the original margin; thick, haphazard rope-like collagen. A hypertrophic scar stays within the wound margin.","k":"Keloid.","a":["keloid","keloid scar"],"rn":[["","Keloid Scar","Keloid Scar","Scar tissue extending beyond the margin of the original injury; Firm, smooth nodules"]]},{"id":16045,"t":"Pathology of the Dermis","f":"sk54","s":"A small red-purple raised lesion on the lip shows many blood vessels grouped together in the dermis. What is it?","o":["Haemangioma – a benign tumour of vessels","Haemangioma – a malignant tumour of vessels","Melanoma – atypical melanocytes","Keloid – haphazard collagen","SCC – keratin pearls"],"e":"<b>Haemangiomas</b>: <b>benign tumours of blood vessels</b>; histology – lots of blood vessels grouped together in the <b>dermis</b>. A dermal condition, like scars.","w":["Benign.","Not.","Not.","Not."],"k":"Haemangioma = benign vascular, dermal.","rn":[["","Haemangioma","Haemangioma","Benign tumour of blood vessels"],["","Image card: What are these red lesions?","What are these red lesions?","Haemangiomas; Benign tumours of blood vessels"]]},{"id":16046,"t":"Pathology of the Dermis","s":"Which pair are both dermal conditions?","o":["Scars and haemangiomas","Psoriasis and seborrhoeic keratosis","Blisters and psoriasis","Seb K and scars","Psoriasis and haemangioma"],"e":"Epidermal: <b>psoriasis</b>, <b>seborrhoeic keratosis</b>, blisters. Dermal: <b>scars</b>, <b>haemangiomas</b>.","w":["Epidermal.","Epidermal.","Mixed.","Mixed."],"k":"Dermal: scars, haemangiomas.","rn":[["","What does a scar show in the dermis?","What does a scar show in the dermis?","Lots of collagen"],["","Image card: What does this histology show?","What does this histology show?","Haemangioma; Lots of blood vessels grouped in the dermis"],["","Which cells form a scar?","Which cells form a scar?","Fibroblasts"]]},{"id":16047,"t":"Pathology of the Dermis","s":"Which of the following is <b>not</b> a function of skin listed?","o":["Production of red blood cells","Vitamin D metabolism","Anti-microbial defence","Attraction/social function","Absorption/secretion"],"e":"Functions: covering; protection (waterproofing); anti-microbial defence; thermoregulation; sensing (temperature, touch, pain); attraction/social; absorption/secretion; <b>vitamin D</b> metabolism.","w":["Listed.","Listed.","Listed.","Listed."],"k":"Skin functions list.","rn":[["","Image card: What are these red lesions?","What are these red lesions?","Haemangiomas; Benign tumours of blood vessels"],["","Why is psoriasis red?","Why is psoriasis red?","Dilated blood vessels high up near the keratin"]]},{"id":16048,"t":"Function of the Skin","f":"sk57","s":"What does the scanning electron micrograph of surface squamous cells illustrate?","o":["Flattened keratinocytes overlap like scales, forming a barrier","Flattened melanocytes overlap like scales, forming a barrier","Sweat duct openings across the skin surface","Collagen bundles beneath the epidermis","Blood vessels in the papillary dermis"],"e":"Flattened surface keratinocytes look like <b>scales</b>, forming <b>overlapping plates</b>: a good <b>barrier and covering</b>.","w":["Keratinocytes.","Not.","Not.","Not."],"k":"Surface keratinocytes = overlapping plates.","rn":[["","Why are surface keratinocytes a good barrier?","Why are surface keratinocytes a good barrier?","Flattened, like overlapping scales/plates"]]},{"id":16049,"t":"Function of the Skin","s":"Which statement about human thermoregulation is correct?","o":["Homeotherms: temperature ≈ production − loss; skin key for loss","Poikilotherms: temperature ≈ production − loss; skin key for loss","Homeotherms: temperature ≈ production + loss; skin key for production","Homeotherms: temperature set by skin alone, without the hypothalamus","Homeotherms: skin plays little role"],"e":"Humans are <b>homeotherms</b>, maintaining temperature above ambient within a narrow range. Body temperature, simply, = <b>heat production − heat loss</b>. Control starts at the <b>hypothalamus</b>, but the skin has a major role, especially <b>heat loss</b>. Hypo- or hyperthermia can be fatal.","w":["Homeotherms.","Minus.","Hypothalamus.","Key role."],"k":"Homeotherm: production − loss.","rn":[["","What is body temperature, simply put?","What is body temperature, simply put?","Heat production minus heat loss"]]},{"id":16050,"t":"Dermis","s":"A runner overheats. Name the coiled gland labelled <b>E</b>, found all over the body and key for thermoregulation.","e":"E is an eccrine sweat gland: blue cuboidal cells with myoepithelium, opening onto the skin, found everywhere; key for temperature control. Apocrine glands (axilla, groin, nipple) open into follicles and cause body odour.","k":"E = eccrine sweat gland.","img":{"src":"16/block","cap":"Block diagram of skin. Labels removed."},"a":["eccrine sweat gland","eccrine gland","eccrine","sweat gland","sweat glands"],"rn":[["","What are the 2 types of sweat gland?","What are the 2 types of sweat gland?","Eccrine; Apocrine"],["","Sweat Gland","Sweat Gland","Coiled tubular structure; Produces sweat; Eccrine or apocrine"],["","Where are eccrine glands found?","Where are eccrine glands found?","All over the body"]]},{"id":16051,"t":"Function of the Skin","f":"sk59","s":"Why does skin have a very well-developed blood flow despite low metabolic activity?","o":["Its main role is maintaining body temperature","Its main role is supplying oxygen to the epidermis","Its main role is delivering melanin to keratinocytes","Its main role is feeding hair follicles","Its main role is removing dead keratin"],"e":"The skin is <b>not very metabolically active</b> yet has a <b>very well developed blood flow</b>, because the primary function of cutaneous circulation is <b>maintenance of body temperature</b>: increased flow (vasodilation) to lose heat, decreased (vasoconstriction) to conserve it. Vessels form superficial and deep vascular plexuses.","w":["Low metabolism.","Not.","Not.","Not."],"k":"Skin blood flow = temperature control.","rn":[["","Why does skin have a well developed blood flow?","Why does skin have a well developed blood flow?","To maintain body temperature; Not for its metabolism"]]},{"id":16052,"t":"Function of the Skin","s":"A runner overheats. Which pair of skin responses increases heat loss?","o":["Vasodilation and sweating","Vasoconstriction and sweating","Vasodilation and piloerection","Vasoconstriction and piloerection","Reduced sweating and vasodilation"],"e":"To lose heat: <b>increased cutaneous blood flow (vasodilation)</b> – heat loss by conduction, convection; <b>sweating</b> – evaporative cooling (latent heat of vaporisation). Vasoconstriction and piloerection (air trapped as insulation; more important in animals) conserve heat.","w":["Constriction conserves.","Piloerection conserves.","Both conserve.","Sweating up."],"k":"Lose heat: vasodilate + sweat.","rn":[["","How does increased blood flow help?","How does increased blood flow help?","Vasodilation; Heat loss by conduction and convection"],["","How does sweating cool you?","How does sweating cool you?","Evaporative cooling; Latent heat of vaporisation"]]},{"id":16053,"t":"Epidermis","s":"Name the dendritic cells labelled <b>G</b> in the basal layer, derived from the neural crest.","e":"G marks melanocytes: neural crest origin, in the basal layer, about 1 per 4 basal cells; skin colour depends on melanin type, melanosome number, spread and breakdown, not melanocyte number.","k":"G = melanocytes.","img":{"src":"16/block","cap":"Block diagram of skin. Labels removed."},"a":["melanocytes","melanocyte"],"rn":[["","What is Melanocytes?","What is Melanocytes?","Dendritic cells from the neural crest; Produce melanin; Not epithelial cells"],["","Why can melanoma occur outside the skin?","Why can melanoma occur outside the skin?","Melanocytes come from neural crest; Found wherever neural crest cells go"],["","What is the ratio of melanocytes to basal cells?","What is the ratio of melanocytes to basal cells?","1 melanocyte per 4 basal cells (1:4)"]]},{"id":16054,"t":"Skin Cancer","s":"Which definition of cancer is correct?","o":["Disorderly cell growth that continues after the stimulus is removed","Disorderly cell growth that stops when the stimulus is removed","Any lump or thickening in the skin","Benign proliferation of melanocytes","Thickening of the epidermis"],"e":"<b>Cancer</b>: abnormal growth of cells which multiply in a disorderly manner and <b>continue</b> after removal of the stimulus. “Cancer has no respect for boundaries.” Skin cancers can be primary or secondary.","w":["Continues.","Not.","Not.","Acanthosis."],"k":"Cancer definition.","rn":[["","Cancer","Cancer","Abnormal growth of cells multiplying in a disorderly manner; Continues after removal of the stimulus"]]},{"id":16055,"t":"Skin Cancer","s":"Which statement about the origins of skin cancers is correct?","o":["The big three are epidermal; others arise elsewhere","All skin cancers arise from the epidermis alone","BCC arises from melanocytes, and melanoma from basal cells","SCC arises from basal cells; BCC from squamous cells","Melanoma arises from keratinocytes"],"e":"SCC – squamous cells; BCC – basal cells; melanoma – melanocytes (all epidermal). But some arise from <b>adnexal structures</b> (sweat glands, hair follicles), <b>mesenchymal elements</b> (vessels, fat) or <b>lymphocytes</b>. If you can name a cell type, there is probably a cancer of it.","w":["Others exist.","Swapped.","Swapped.","Melanocytes."],"k":"Skin cancer origins."},{"id":16056,"t":"Skin Cancer","f":"sk66","s":"Which cancers are recognised to metastasise to the skin?","o":["Melanoma, lung, colon, breast, ovarian, oral SCC","Melanoma, lung, colon, breast, ovarian, BCC","Prostate and bladder only","BCC and haemangioma only","None; cancers do not spread to skin"],"e":"Cancers recognised to metastasise to skin: <b>melanoma, lung, colon, breast, ovarian, SCC of the oral cavity</b>. Notes: colon frequently metastasises to the <b>liver</b>; lung to the <b>adrenal gland</b>.","w":["BCC rarely spreads.","More.","Not.","They do."],"k":"Skin metastases list.","rn":[["","What are the 6 cancers recognised to metastasise to skin?","What are the 6 cancers recognised to metastasise to skin?","Melanoma; Lung; Colon; Breast; Ovarian; SCC of oral cavity"]]},{"id":16057,"t":"Pathology of the Epidermis","s":"These well-demarcated, raised red plaques with silvery scale on the trunk and knees are caused by rapid epidermal turnover. Name the condition.","e":"Psoriasis: plaques with silvery scale; histology shows acanthosis, thick keratin and dilated vessels near the surface.","k":"Psoriasis.","img":{"src":"16/psoriasis","cap":"Two patients with the same condition."},"a":["psoriasis","plaque psoriasis"],"rn":[["","Image card: What is this skin condition?","What is this skin condition?","Psoriasis; Well demarcated red plaques; Silvery scale"],["","What does psoriasis look like clinically?","What does psoriasis look like clinically?","Well demarcated raised plaques; Silvery scale; Erythematous (red)"]]},{"id":16058,"t":"Skin Cancer","s":"An elderly farmer has a slowly enlarging pearly nodule on the nose with superficial vessels, which has eroded into nearby cartilage. Nodes are normal. What is the likely diagnosis and expected behaviour?","o":["BCC – locally infiltrative, rarely metastasises","BCC – locally infiltrative, often metastasises to nodes","SCC – frequently metastasises to brain","Melanoma – high metastatic propensity","Seborrhoeic keratosis – benign"],"e":"<b>BCC</b>: <b>most common</b> skin cancer in the Western world; commonly <b>head and neck</b>; <b>infiltrates locally</b> but <b>rarely spreads</b>; multiple; recurs; can be <b>disfiguring</b>; caused by <b>UV</b>. Clinically: <b>nodules</b> or ulcerated nodules, <b>pearly</b> with <b>superficial blood vessels</b>; can be pigmented. Also called a <b>rodent ulcer</b> because it gnaws locally.","w":["Rarely.","Pearly, local.","Not.","Not."],"k":"BCC: pearly, local, rarely spreads.","rn":[["","What are the 7 features of BCC?","What are the 7 features of BCC?","Most common skin cancer; Head and neck; Infiltrates locally, rarely spreads; Tends to be multiple; Tends to recur; Can be disfiguring on the face; Caused by UV"],["","Does BCC metastasise?","Does BCC metastasise?","Rarely; Infiltrates locally"],["","Why is BCC prognosis better than SCC and melanoma?","Why is BCC prognosis better than SCC and melanoma?","Rarely metastasises"]]},{"id":16059,"t":"Skin Cancer","f":"sk69","s":"Why is BCC called a “rodent ulcer”?","o":["It gnaws away at surrounding tissue","It is caused by rodent bites","It spreads through lymph like rodents","It is small, like a mouse","It is seen mainly in rodents"],"e":"<b>Rodent ulcer</b>: locally infiltrative, it “<b>eats away</b>” at surrounding tissue (e.g. destroying the ear, invading bone or the eye socket) – <b>locally destructive</b>, though rarely metastasising, giving a <b>better prognosis</b> than SCC or melanoma.","w":["Not.","Rarely spreads.","Not.","Not."],"k":"Rodent ulcer = gnaws locally.","rn":[["","Why is BCC called a rodent ulcer?","Why is BCC called a rodent ulcer?","Locally infiltrative; \"Eats away\" at surrounding tissue"]]},{"id":16060,"t":"Skin Cancer","f":"sk73","s":"Which histological features suggest BCC?","o":["Blue nests on the epidermis, palisading, clefts","Pink nests attached to epidermis, keratin pearls","Blue nests separate from the epidermis, with keratin pearls","Atypical melanocytes with dusty melanin","Thick haphazard collagen bundles"],"e":"<b>BCC</b>: “<b>blue</b>” cells in the dermis, <b>attached to overlying epithelium</b>; lots of appearances. From the recording: <b>clefts</b> between nests and dermis, and <b>peripheral palisading</b> (cells at the edge line up like a picket fence). Keratin is rarely found.","w":["SCC.","Attached; no pearls.","Melanoma.","Keloid."],"k":"BCC: blue, palisading, clefts.","rn":[["","Image card: What skin cancer is this? Name 2 features.","What skin cancer is this? Name 2 features.","Basal cell carcinoma; Blue cells; Peripheral palisading, clefting"],["","What are 2 other histology features of BCC?","What are 2 other histology features of BCC?","Peripheral palisading; Clefting"],["","Image card: What skin cancer is this? How can you tell?","What skin cancer is this? How can you tell?","Basal cell carcinoma; Blue nests in the dermis; Attached to overlying epidermis"]]},{"id":16061,"t":"Skin Cancer","s":"A kidney transplant recipient develops a crusted, horn-like lesion on the ear, and later enlarged neck lymph nodes that show the same tumour. What is the most likely diagnosis?","o":["Squamous cell carcinoma","Basal cell carcinoma","Melanoma","Seborrhoeic keratosis","Keloid"],"e":"<b>SCC</b>: second most common; <b>propensity to metastasise</b> (lymph nodes, lung, liver, brain), so prognosis is <b>worse than BCC</b>; head and neck (sun exposed). Typically <b>hyperkeratotic</b>, sometimes like a <b>horn</b>. Risk factors include <b>immunosuppression</b> (transplant, immunosuppressant drugs). Node spread is not expected with BCC.","w":["Rarely spreads.","Not horn-like.","Benign.","Scar."],"k":"SCC: horn-like, metastasises; transplant risk.","rn":[["","Image card: What skin cancer is this? What features?","What skin cancer is this? What features?","Squamous cell carcinoma; Hyperkeratotic; Can form a horn"],["","SCC","SCC","Squamous cell carcinoma"],["","Image card: What skin cancer is this?","What skin cancer is this?","Squamous cell carcinoma"]]},{"id":16062,"t":"Skin Cancer","s":"Which of these is <b>not</b> listed as a risk factor for SCC?","o":["More than 100 moles","Arsenic","Xeroderma pigmentosum","Smoking","Immunosuppression"],"e":"SCC risk factors: <b>UV</b>; previous skin cancer or pre-malignant lesion; fair skin; <b>immunosuppression</b>; <b>xeroderma pigmentosum</b>; <b>smoking</b>; <b>arsenic</b>; <b>ageing</b> (recording also: chronic inflammation, genetic conditions). <b>&gt;100 moles</b> is a <b>melanoma</b> risk factor.","w":["Listed.","Listed.","Listed.","Listed."],"k":">100 moles = melanoma risk.","rn":[["","What are the 10 melanoma risk factors?","What are the 10 melanoma risk factors?","UV radiation; Sunbed use; Skin phototype I/II; Red/blonde hair; Blue/blue-grey eyes; Sunburn; >100 moles; Family history; Crohn's disease; HIV/AIDS"]]},{"id":16063,"t":"Skin Cancer","f":"sk81","s":"Why does SCC look pink on histology with whorls of keratin?","o":["Squamous cells mature into keratin, giving pink pearls","Squamous cells produce melanin, giving pink pearls","Basal cells mature into keratin, giving blue pearls","Squamous cells produce collagen, giving pink whorls","It is a vascular tumour"],"e":"Squamous cells mature into <b>keratin</b>, so SCC produces <b>lots of keratin</b> – pink whorls (<b>keratin pearls</b>) and <b>glassy pink</b> tumour cells – not blue like BCC.","w":["Keratin.","Squamous.","Not.","Not."],"k":"SCC: pink keratin pearls.","rn":[["","Image card: What skin cancer is this? How can you tell?","What skin cancer is this? How can you tell?","Squamous cell carcinoma; Pink, glassy cells; Producing keratin"],["","What does SCC look like on histology?","What does SCC look like on histology?","Pink, glassy cells; Producing lots of keratin"]]},{"id":16064,"t":"Skin Cancer","s":"If an SCC's keratin horn falls off, what might it be confused with clinically?","o":["A BCC","A haemangioma","A keloid","Psoriasis","A sebaceous gland"],"e":"From the recording: SCCs are typically hyperkeratotic, sometimes horn-like; if the horn falls off it can <b>look like a BCC</b>. On darker skin it may look unassuming, so recognise features of “bad” lesions.","w":["Not stated.","Not stated.","Not stated.","Not stated."],"k":"SCC without horn may mimic BCC.","rn":[["","BCC vs SCC: metastasis","BCC vs SCC: metastasis","BCC: rarely; SCC: can metastasise"],["","BCC vs SCC vs melanoma: colour on histology","BCC vs SCC vs melanoma: colour on histology","BCC: blue; SCC: pink (keratin); Melanoma: brown (melanin)"],["","What is the order of skin cancer frequency?","What is the order of skin cancer frequency?","BCC most common; Then SCC; Then melanoma"]]},{"id":16065,"t":"Skin Cancer","s":"Which statistics about melanoma in the UK are correct?","o":["5th commonest cancer; 20th cause of cancer death; up 135% since 1990","1st commonest cancer; 5th cause of cancer death; up 135% since 1990","5th commonest cancer; 20th cause of death; down 35% since 1990","20th commonest cancer; 5th cause of cancer death; up 135%","5th commonest cancer; no recorded deaths"],"e":"Melanoma: <b>5th most common cancer</b> in the UK; <b>20th most common cause of cancer death</b>; rates up <b>135% since 1990</b>; approximately <b>1000 deaths in 2017</b> (the lecturer said current figures suggest about 2,600 deaths per year). Most on <b>trunk and legs</b>; most common in <b>fair skin</b>.","w":["5th.","Rising.","Swapped.","~1000 deaths."],"k":"Melanoma UK statistics.","rn":[["","Where does melanoma rank among UK cancers?","Where does melanoma rank among UK cancers?","5th most common"]]},{"id":16066,"t":"Skin Cancer","s":"Which site distribution is typical for melanoma compared with BCC and SCC?","o":["Melanoma: trunk and legs; BCC/SCC: head and neck","Melanoma: head and neck; BCC/SCC: trunk and legs","Melanoma: trunk only; BCC/SCC: legs only","All three mainly on the trunk","All three mainly on the hands"],"e":"Melanoma mostly on the <b>trunk and legs</b>; BCC and SCC commonly on the <b>head and neck</b> (sun-exposed).","w":["Swapped.","Not.","Different.","Not."],"k":"Melanoma: trunk/legs.","rn":[["","Where do most melanomas occur?","Where do most melanomas occur?","Trunk and legs"],["","Where does SCC commonly occur?","Where does SCC commonly occur?","Head and neck (sun exposed areas)"],["","Where does BCC commonly occur?","Where does BCC commonly occur?","Head and neck"]]},{"id":16067,"t":"Pathology of the Epidermis","s":"An older adult has many “stuck-on cornflake” brown lesions on the back. Name the benign lesion, important because it can mimic melanoma.","e":"Seborrhoeic keratosis: benign, “stuck on”; histology shows keratin-filled nests within the epidermis. Clinically it can be hard to tell from melanoma.","k":"Seborrhoeic keratosis.","img":{"src":"16/sebk","cap":"Lesions in an older adult."},"a":["seborrhoeic keratosis","seborrheic keratosis","seborrhoeic keratoses","seb k","seborrheic keratoses","basal cell papilloma"],"rn":[["","What does seborrhoeic keratosis show on histology?","What does seborrhoeic keratosis show on histology?","Acanthosis; Nests of keratin within the epidermis"],["","Image card: What condition shows this histology?","What condition shows this histology?","Seborrhoeic keratosis; Acanthosis; Keratin nests within the epidermis"]]},{"id":16068,"t":"Skin Cancer","s":"Which statement about melanoma types is correct?","o":["All types are malignant – there is no benign melanoma","Superficial spreading is benign; nodular is malignant","Lentigo maligna and lentigo maligna melanoma are identical","Amelanotic melanoma is always black","Nodular melanoma is benign"],"e":"Types: superficial spreading, nodular, acral lentiginous, subungual, lentigo maligna melanoma – <b>all malignant</b>, <b>no benign melanoma</b>. <b>Lentigo maligna</b> differs from <b>lentigo maligna melanoma</b>. <b>Amelanotic melanoma</b> does not produce melanin, so it doesn't look dark.","w":["All malignant.","Different.","Not pigmented.","Malignant."],"k":"No benign melanoma.","rn":[["","Is there a benign melanoma?","Is there a benign melanoma?","No, all melanomas are malignant"],["","What are the 5 types of melanoma?","What are the 5 types of melanoma?","Superficial spreading; Nodular; Acral lentiginous; Subungual; Lentigo maligna"],["","Image card: What type of melanoma is this?","What type of melanoma is this?","Lentigo maligna melanoma"]]},{"id":16069,"t":"Skin Cancer","f":"sk86","s":"A mole has become asymmetrical with an irregular border, variable colour, 8 mm across, and has changed recently. Which tool captures these features?","o":["ABCDE: asymmetry, border, colour, diameter >6 mm, evolving","ABCDE: asymmetry, border, colour, depth >6 mm, elevation","Breslow thickness","Fitzpatrick scale","The surgical sieve"],"e":"<b>ABCDE</b>: <b>A</b>symmetry, <b>B</b>order, <b>C</b>olour, <b>D</b>iameter &gt;6 mm, <b>E</b>volving – a way everyone can check moles. Clinicians worried about melanoma <b>excise</b> the lesion for histopathology.","w":["Diameter, evolving.","Histology.","UV response.","Classification."],"k":"ABCDE mole check.","rn":[["","Image card: What type of melanoma is this, and what is ABCDE?","What type of melanoma is this, and what is ABCDE?","Superficial spreading melanoma; Asymmetry, Border, Colour, Diameter >6 mm, Evolving"],["","In ABCDE, what do the letters look for in a mole?","In ABCDE, what do the letters look for in a mole?","Asymmetrical shape; Irregular border; Variable or darker colour; Diameter over 6 mm; Evolving (changing)"]]},{"id":16070,"t":"Skin Cancer","s":"A patient with very dark skin presents with a pigmented lesion on the sole of the foot. Why should melanoma be considered?","o":["Acral sites are at risk in all phototypes","Melanoma never occurs in darker skin","The sole has no melanocytes","Only UV-exposed sites develop melanoma in darker skin","Acral lesions are always benign"],"e":"Key points: melanoma occurs at <b>any age</b>; the fairer the skin the more at risk (I–III), but <b>acral sites are at risk in higher skin phototypes</b>. Acral lentiginous melanoma (sole, toes) and subungual melanoma are types.","w":["It does.","It has.","Acral.","Not."],"k":"Acral melanoma in darker skin.","rn":[["","Which sites are at risk in higher skin phototypes?","Which sites are at risk in higher skin phototypes?","Acral sites"]]},{"id":16071,"t":"Skin Cancer","s":"What is Breslow thickness and how does it relate to prognosis?","o":["Granular layer to deepest tumour point (mm); >4 mm poor","Surface to deepest tumour point (mm); >4 mm excellent","Granular layer to deepest point (mm); <1 mm poor","Width of lesion in mm; >6 mm poor","Mitoses per mm²; >4 poor"],"e":"<b>Breslow thickness</b>: from the <b>granular cell layer to the deepest point</b> of the tumour, in <b>mm</b>. <b>&lt;1 mm</b>: small metastatic risk, prognosis <b>excellent</b>; <b>&gt;4 mm</b>: high risk, prognosis <b>poor</b>.","w":["Poor.","Excellent.","Diameter.","Not."],"k":"Breslow: granular layer → deepest point.","rn":[["","Breslow Thickness","Breslow Thickness","From granular cell layer to deepest point of tumour; Measured in mm"]]},{"id":16072,"t":"Skin Cancer","f":"sk92","s":"Which histological features characterise melanoma at high power?","o":["Atypical melanocytes, prominent nucleoli, dusty melanin","Typical melanocytes, small nucleoli, dusty melanin","Blue palisading nests attached to epidermis","Keratin pearls and glassy pink cells","Foamy sebocytes"],"e":"Melanoma histology: <b>highly atypical melanocytes</b>, <b>prominent nucleoli</b>, <b>dusty melanin pigmentation</b> (brown).","w":["Atypical.","BCC.","SCC.","Sebaceous."],"k":"Melanoma: atypical melanocytes, nucleoli, dusty melanin.","rn":[["","Image card: What skin cancer is this? Name 3 features.","What skin cancer is this? Name 3 features.","Melanoma; Highly atypical melanocytes; Prominent nucleoli; Dusty melanin pigmentation"],["","What does melanoma look like on histology?","What does melanoma look like on histology?","Highly atypical melanocytes; Prominent nucleoli; Dusty melanin pigmentation"]]},{"id":16073,"t":"Skin Cancer","f":"sk94","s":"Three skin tumours are biopsied: A is blue with palisading; B is pink with keratin; C has brown pigment and atypical melanocytes. Rank them from best to worst prognosis, generally speaking.","o":["A (BCC) → B (SCC) → C (melanoma)","B (SCC) → A (BCC) → C (melanoma)","C (melanoma) → B (SCC) → A (BCC)","A (BCC) → C (melanoma) → B (SCC)","All have equal prognosis"],"e":"<b>BCC</b> (blue): most common, local, best prognosis. <b>SCC</b> (pink, keratin): can metastasise, worse than BCC. <b>Melanoma</b> (brown, melanin): high metastatic propensity, worse than BCC/SCC (generally). Memory aid: BCC = Blue, SCC = pink keratin, Melanoma = Melanin brown.","w":["BCC best.","Reversed.","Melanoma worst.","Different."],"k":"Prognosis: BCC > SCC > melanoma.","rn":[["","BCC vs SCC vs melanoma: colour on histology","BCC vs SCC vs melanoma: colour on histology","BCC: blue; SCC: pink (keratin); Melanoma: brown (melanin)"],["","Is melanoma worse than BCC and SCC?","Is melanoma worse than BCC and SCC?","Generally, yes; Propensity to metastasise"]]},{"id":16074,"t":"Skin Cancer","s":"Which ordering of skin cancer frequency is correct?","o":["BCC, then SCC, then melanoma","SCC, then BCC, then melanoma","Melanoma, then BCC, then SCC","BCC, then melanoma, then SCC","All equally common"],"e":"Most common: <b>BCC</b>, then <b>SCC</b>, then <b>melanoma</b>.","w":["BCC first.","Melanoma least.","SCC second.","Different."],"k":"BCC > SCC > melanoma frequency.","rn":[["","What is the order of skin cancer frequency?","What is the order of skin cancer frequency?","BCC most common; Then SCC; Then melanoma"]]},{"id":16075,"t":"Skin Cancer","s":"From which epidermal cells does SCC arise?","o":["Squamous cells – keratinocytes above the basal layer","Basal cells – the proliferative layer of the epidermis","Melanocytes in the basal layer","Langerhans cells in the spinous layer","Merkel cells in the basal layer"],"e":"SCC arises from the <b>squamous cells</b>, i.e. every other keratinocyte apart from the basal cells.","w":["BCC.","Melanoma.","Not.","Merkel carcinoma."],"k":"SCC from squamous keratinocytes.","rn":[["","Where does SCC arise from?","Where does SCC arise from?","Squamous cells (keratinocytes)"]]},{"id":16076,"t":"Pathology of the Epidermis","s":"Name the descriptive term for a raised solid skin lesion measuring 1.5 cm.","e":"Nodule: raised, solid, >1 cm; papule ≤1 cm.","k":"Nodule.","a":["nodule"],"rn":[["","Nodule","Nodule","Raised solid lesion; >1 cm"]]},{"id":16077,"t":"Pathology of the Epidermis","s":"Name the descriptive term for a fluid-filled blister measuring 8 mm.","e":"Bulla: fluid-filled, >5 mm; vesicle ≤5 mm.","k":"Bulla.","a":["bulla","bullae"],"rn":[["","Bulla","Bulla","Fluid filled space within/below epidermis; >5 mm"]]},{"id":16078,"t":"Pathology of the Epidermis","s":"A flat, red area of discolouration measuring 3 cm is best termed a:","o":["Patch","Macule","Plaque","Nodule","Vesicle"],"e":"<b>Macule</b>: flat discolouration &lt;1 cm; <b>patch</b>: &gt;1 cm.","w":["<1 cm.","Raised.","Raised.","Fluid."],"k":"Patch = flat >1 cm.","rn":[["","Patch","Patch","Flat area of discolouration; >1 cm"]]},{"id":16079,"t":"Pathology of the Epidermis","s":"Psoriasis lesions were described as raised and well-demarcated with silvery scale. Which descriptive term best fits a raised plateau-like lesion over 1 cm?","o":["Plaque","Macule","Vesicle","Wheal","Papule"],"e":"<b>Plaque</b>: raised lesion forming a <b>plateau-like</b> elevation &gt;1 cm – as in psoriasis plaques.","w":["Flat.","Fluid.","Transitory.","<1 cm."],"k":"Plaque = plateau >1 cm.","rn":[["","Plaque","Plaque","Raised plateau-like lesion; >1 cm"]]},{"id":16080,"t":"Epidermis","s":"An autoimmune disease destroys desmosomes. Where would the blister form and which layer's “prickles” are lost?","o":["Intraepidermal, in the stratum spinosum","Subepidermal, beneath the basement membrane","Intraepidermal, in the stratum corneum only","In the dermis between collagen fibres","In the subcutis"],"e":"Desmosomes form the <b>prickles</b> of the <b>stratum spinosum</b> joining keratinocytes. Antibodies to desmosomes cause an <b>intraepidermal</b> split; hemidesmosome antibodies cause a subepidermal split.","w":["Hemidesmosomes.","Spinosum.","Not.","Not."],"k":"Desmosome loss → intraepidermal blister.","rn":[["","What is Spongiosis?","What is Spongiosis?","Fluid seeps between keratinocytes in the stratum spinosum; Prickles become more obvious"],["","What cells make up the stratum spinosum?","What cells make up the stratum spinosum?","Polyhedral squamous cells (keratinocytes)"],["","What are the 4 layers of the epidermis (surface to deep)?","What are the 4 layers of the epidermis (surface to deep)?","Stratum corneum (horn cell); Stratum granulosum (granular cell); Stratum spinosum (prickle cell); Stratum basale (basal cell)"]]},{"id":16081,"t":"Epidermis","s":"A carcinoma in situ is confined above an intact basement membrane. What feature must appear before it is called an invasive carcinoma?","o":["Breach of the basement membrane","Increased keratin on the surface","Spongiosis between keratinocytes","More melanocytes appearing in the basal layer","Dilated vessels near the surface"],"e":"Before calling it carcinoma, it must have <b>invaded</b>: atypical cells <b>breach</b> the BM (cancer cells can dissolve BM collagen) into the dermis.","w":["Not.","Not.","Not.","Psoriasis."],"k":"Invasion = BM breach.","rn":[["","What happens if atypical cells breach the basement membrane?","What happens if atypical cells breach the basement membrane?","Invasion; In situ becomes invasive cancer"],["","How do cancer cells breach the basement membrane?","How do cancer cells breach the basement membrane?","Produce proteins that dissolve the collagen"]]},{"id":16082,"t":"Epidermis","s":"Which sequence correctly describes keratinocyte maturation?","o":["Plump basal → polyhedral spinous → flat granular → anucleate corneum (~28 days)","Plump basal → flat granular → polyhedral spinous → anucleate corneum (~28 days)","Anucleate corneum → granular → spinous → basal (~28 days)","Plump basal → polyhedral spinous → flat granular → anucleate corneum (~2 days)","Granular cells divide to form the basal layer"],"e":"Basal cells (proliferative, plump, big nuclei, blue) mature upward: they get smaller, flatter, nucleus shrinks (spinosum → granulosum), then die (anucleate corneum) and are shed (~28 days in recording; ~20–40 days in notes).","w":["Spinous before granular.","Upwards.","~28 days.","Basal divides."],"k":"Keratinocyte maturation path.","rn":[["","What are the 4 layers of the epidermis (surface to deep)?","What are the 4 layers of the epidermis (surface to deep)?","Stratum corneum (horn cell); Stratum granulosum (granular cell); Stratum spinosum (prickle cell); Stratum basale (basal cell)"],["","Image card: Name the 4 layers marked by the arrows, top to bottom.","Name the 4 layers marked by the arrows, top to bottom.","Horn cell layer (stratum corneum); Granular cell layer (stratum granulosum); Prickle cell layer (stratum spinosum); Basal cell layer (stratum basale)"]]},{"id":16083,"t":"Epidermis","s":"A patient's biopsy shows an increased number of melanocytes along the basal layer. How should this be interpreted?","o":["Pathological – colour is not about melanocyte number","Normal, simply reflecting a darker natural skin colour","Normal, reflecting acral skin","Spongiosis of the basal layer","Langerhans cell hyperplasia"],"e":"An <b>increased number of melanocytes is pathological</b>; normal colour variation depends on melanin type, melanosome number, distribution and degradation.","w":["Not number.","Not.","Not.","Not."],"k":"More melanocytes = pathological.","rn":[["","What does an increase in melanocyte number suggest?","What does an increase in melanocyte number suggest?","A pathological condition"]]},{"id":16084,"t":"Epidermis","s":"A lesion biopsy contains a dark-brown immunostain highlighting cells along the base of the epidermis. What is being shown?","o":["Melanocytes, by melanA immunostain","Langerhans cells, shown by H&E","The basement membrane, shown by PAS","Collagen, shown by trichrome","Merkel cells, shown by H&E"],"e":"MelanA immunohistochemistry highlights <b>melanocytes</b> dark brown along the base (you don't need to know the stain name; it shows distribution).","w":["Not.","Pink line.","Not.","Not seen."],"k":"MelanA = melanocytes."},{"id":16085,"t":"Pathology of the Epidermis","s":"Which explanation best links psoriasis's redness to its histology?","o":["Dilated vessels high up near the keratin","Dilated vessels deep in the dermis","Loss of melanin from basal cells","Spongiosis between keratinocytes","Grouped vessels forming a haemangioma"],"e":"Psoriasis redness comes from <b>dilated blood vessels</b> high up near the keratin layer.","w":["High up.","Not.","Eczema.","Not."],"k":"Psoriasis redness = dilated vessels.","rn":[["","Why is psoriasis red?","Why is psoriasis red?","Dilated blood vessels high up near the keratin"]]},{"id":16086,"t":"Dermis","s":"Acne is centred on which skin structure?","o":["The pilosebaceous unit","The eccrine sweat gland","The apocrine sweat gland","The basement membrane","The subcutis"],"e":"Diseases like <b>acne</b> are centred on the <b>pilosebaceous unit</b> (hair follicle + sebaceous gland + arrector pili).","w":["Not.","Not.","Not.","Not."],"k":"Acne → pilosebaceous unit.","rn":[["","Which disease centres on the pilosebaceous unit?","Which disease centres on the pilosebaceous unit?","Acne"]]},{"id":16087,"t":"Epidermis","s":"Which cell in the skin is not epithelial in origin?","o":["Melanocyte","Keratinocyte","Basal cell","Spinous cell","Granular cell"],"e":"Melanocytes are <b>dendritic cells from the neural crest</b>; they are <b>not epithelial</b>. The others are keratinocytes at different stages.","w":["Epithelial.","Epithelial.","Epithelial.","Epithelial."],"k":"Melanocytes: neural crest, non-epithelial.","rn":[["","What is the ratio of melanocytes to basal cells?","What is the ratio of melanocytes to basal cells?","1 melanocyte per 4 basal cells (1:4)"]]},{"id":16088,"t":"Epidermis","s":"Which pairing of cell and sensation/immune role is correct?","o":["Merkel – sustained touch; Langerhans – antigen presentation","Merkel – antigen presentation; Langerhans – sustained touch","Melanocyte – sustained touch; Langerhans – pigment","Keratinocyte – antigen presentation; Merkel – pigment","Merkel – pigment; Langerhans – touch"],"e":"<b>Merkel</b>: slowly adapting mechanoreceptors (sustained touch). <b>Langerhans</b>: antigen-presenting surveillance.","w":["Swapped.","Not.","Not.","Not."],"k":"Merkel touch; Langerhans immune.","rn":[["","What is Langerhans Cells?","What is Langerhans Cells?","Dendritic antigen presenting cells; Immune surveillance of the skin"],["","What sensation do Merkel cells help with?","What sensation do Merkel cells help with?","Sustained touch / pressure"],["","What do Langerhans cells do?","What do Langerhans cells do?","Sample antigens; Present them to immune cells in lymph nodes"]]},{"id":16089,"t":"Epidermis","s":"An autoimmune disease destroys desmosomes. Name the layer labelled <b>C</b>, whose “prickles” would be lost.","e":"C is the stratum spinosum: desmosomes give the “prickles”; loss of desmosomes gives an intraepidermal blister.","k":"C = stratum spinosum.","img":{"src":"16/layers","cap":"Epidermis, H&amp;E. Labels removed."},"a":["stratum spinosum","spinosum","prickle cell layer","spinous layer","prickle layer"],"rn":[["","What is Spongiosis?","What is Spongiosis?","Fluid seeps between keratinocytes in the stratum spinosum; Prickles become more obvious"],["","What are the 4 layers of the epidermis (surface to deep)?","What are the 4 layers of the epidermis (surface to deep)?","Stratum corneum (horn cell); Stratum granulosum (granular cell); Stratum spinosum (prickle cell); Stratum basale (basal cell)"],["","Image card: Name the 4 layers marked by the arrows, top to bottom.","Name the 4 layers marked by the arrows, top to bottom.","Horn cell layer (stratum corneum); Granular cell layer (stratum granulosum); Prickle cell layer (stratum spinosum); Basal cell layer (stratum basale)"]]},{"id":16090,"t":"Dermis","s":"Which eccrine gland feature contrasts with apocrine?","o":["Eccrine open onto skin; cuboidal with myoepithelium","Eccrine open into hair follicles and are lined by columnar cells","Eccrine use decapitation secretion","Eccrine are confined to the axilla","Eccrine produce body odour"],"e":"<b>Eccrine</b>: open directly onto the skin surface; cuboidal cells with myoepithelial cells; bluer; temperature control; everywhere.","w":["Apocrine.","Apocrine.","Everywhere.","Not."],"k":"Eccrine features.","rn":[["","Where do eccrine glands open?","Where do eccrine glands open?","Directly onto the skin surface"]]},{"id":16091,"t":"Dermis","s":"Which of the dermal ECM components is listed?","o":["GAGs, hyaluronan, proteoglycans","Keratin, melanin and hyaluronan","Haemoglobin and myoglobin","Actin and myosin","DNA and RNA"],"e":"ECM is a <b>hydrated protein gel</b> containing <b>glycosaminoglycans, hyaluronan, proteoglycans</b>.","w":["Epidermal.","Not.","Not.","Not."],"k":"ECM components.","rn":[["","What 3 proteins are in the extracellular matrix?","What 3 proteins are in the extracellular matrix?","Glycosaminoglycans; Hyaluronan; Proteoglycans"]]},{"id":16092,"t":"Dermis","s":"A dermal special stain shows collagen in blue and another stain shows thin, dark, wavy fibres. What is the second?","o":["Elastic fibres","Basement membrane","Melanocytes","Keratin","Langerhans cells"],"e":"Special stains: collagen in <b>blue</b>; <b>elastic fibres</b> as thin dark wavy lines. Special stains help identify dermal components.","w":["PAS pink line.","MelanA.","Epidermal.","Not."],"k":"Elastic fibres: thin dark wavy.","rn":[["","Image card: What do these special stains highlight in the dermis?","What do these special stains highlight in the dermis?","Collagen fibres; Elastic fibres"]]},{"id":16093,"t":"Dermis","s":"Which cell in the dermis forms the scar after injury?","o":["Fibroblasts","Histiocytes","Melanocytes","Sebocytes","Langerhans cells"],"e":"Fibroblasts make collagen and are key in <b>skin repair</b>: after injury, dermal fibroblasts form the <b>scar</b> – hence scars are dermal conditions.","w":["Phagocytes.","Pigment.","Gland.","Immune."],"k":"Fibroblasts form scars.","rn":[["","Which cells form a scar?","Which cells form a scar?","Fibroblasts"]]},{"id":16094,"t":"Dermis","s":"Which statement about histiocytes forming giant cells is correct?","o":["Histiocytes can form giant cells, with associated pathology","Fibroblasts can form giant cells, with associated pathology","Melanocytes can form giant cells","Giant cells arise from basal keratinocytes","Giant cells are always benign"],"e":"Histiocytes: variable size and appearance, bean-shaped nuclei, <b>can form giant cells</b>; also have pathology associated.","w":["Histiocytes.","Not.","Not.","Pathology."],"k":"Histiocytes → giant cells.","rn":[["","What can histiocytes form?","What can histiocytes form?","Giant cells"]]},{"id":16095,"t":"Skin Cancer","s":"Which of the following correctly matches a melanoma type with its typical location?","o":["Subungual – under the nails","Acral lentiginous – face","Lentigo maligna melanoma – soles","Nodular – nails only","Superficial spreading – eye only"],"e":"<b>Subungual</b>: under nails (dark streaks); <b>acral lentiginous</b>: sole/toes; <b>lentigo maligna melanoma</b>: flat brown patches on sun-damaged facial skin; nodular: raised dark nodules.","w":["Soles/toes.","Face.","Raised nodules.","Skin."],"k":"Melanoma types/locations.","rn":[["","Image card: What type of melanoma is this?","What type of melanoma is this?","Subungual melanoma (under the nail)"],["","Subungual","Subungual","Under the nail"]]},{"id":16096,"t":"Skin Cancer","s":"A melanoma is 0.6 mm in Breslow thickness. What prognosis is expected?","o":["Excellent – small metastatic risk","Poor – high metastatic risk","Uncertain – Breslow not applicable","Same as a >4 mm tumour","Fatal within a year"],"e":"<b>&lt;1 mm</b>: risk of metastasis small; prognosis <b>excellent</b>. &gt;4 mm: high risk; poor.","w":["<1 mm.","Applies.","Different.","Not."],"k":"<1 mm excellent.","rn":[["","Breslow <1 mm: prognosis?","Breslow <1 mm: prognosis?","Small risk of metastasis; Excellent prognosis"]]},{"id":16097,"t":"Skin Cancer","s":"Which of the following would most likely be seen in a cutaneous metastasis from colon adenocarcinoma?","o":["Tumour glands in the dermis beneath normal epidermis","Tumour glands within the epidermis, replacing keratinocytes","Blue nests attached to the epidermis","Keratin pearls arising from the epidermis","Atypical melanocytes in the basal layer"],"e":"The cutaneous metastasis slide shows <b>glands of metastatic tumour in the dermis beneath normal epidermis</b>.","w":["Dermis.","BCC.","SCC.","Melanoma."],"k":"Metastasis: dermal glands, normal epidermis.","rn":[["","Image card: This is a tumour in the dermis. What type of skin cancer is it?","This is a tumour in the dermis. What type of skin cancer is it?","Cutaneous metastasis (secondary)"]]},{"id":16098,"t":"Skin Cancer","s":"Elderly patients have slowly growing pearly nodules and ulcers on the face, one eroding the nose, with normal lymph nodes. Name the tumour.","e":"Basal cell carcinoma (“rodent ulcer”): locally infiltrative, rarely metastasises; histology shows blue nests attached to the epidermis with palisading and clefts. The commonest skin cancer.","k":"BCC.","img":{"src":"16/bcc","cap":"Lesions on sun-exposed skin of older adults."},"a":["basal cell carcinoma","bcc","rodent ulcer"],"rn":[["","BCC","BCC","Basal cell carcinoma"],["","Image card: What skin cancer is this? Name 2 features.","What skin cancer is this? Name 2 features.","Basal cell carcinoma; Blue cells; Peripheral palisading, clefting"],["","Image card: What skin cancer is this? How can you tell?","What skin cancer is this? How can you tell?","Basal cell carcinoma; Blue nests in the dermis; Attached to overlying epidermis"]]},{"id":16099,"t":"Epidermis","s":"Which statement about appearances in different skin types is correct?","o":["Conditions can look very different on darker skin","Conditions look identical across all skin types","Darker skin never develops skin cancer","BCC is never pigmented","SCC always looks obvious"],"e":"Conditions look different in <b>different skin types</b>; BCC can be pigmented; SCC may look unassuming on darker skin.","w":["Different.","It does.","Can be.","Unassuming."],"k":"Appearance varies by skin type.","rn":[["","Does darker skin mean more melanocytes?","Does darker skin mean more melanocytes?","No"]]},{"id":16100,"t":"Skin Cancer","s":"An immunosuppressed transplant recipient has crusted, keratotic ulcerated lesions on the lip and ear and later develops enlarged neck nodes. Name the tumour.","e":"Squamous cell carcinoma: from keratinocytes above the basal layer; pink with keratin pearls on histology; can metastasise to nodes. Risk factors include UV, arsenic, xeroderma pigmentosum, smoking, immunosuppression.","k":"SCC.","img":{"src":"16/scc","cap":"Lesions on the lip and ear."},"a":["squamous cell carcinoma","scc","cutaneous squamous cell carcinoma"],"rn":[["","SCC","SCC","Squamous cell carcinoma"],["","Image card: What skin cancer is this?","What skin cancer is this?","Squamous cell carcinoma"],["","What are the 3 main types of primary skin cancer?","What are the 3 main types of primary skin cancer?","Squamous cell carcinoma; Basal cell carcinoma; Melanoma"]]},{"id":16101,"t":"Function of the Skin","s":"What does piloerection do and in whom is it more important?","o":["Traps air as insulation; more important in animals","Traps air as insulation; more important in humans","Increases sweating; more important in humans","Dilates vessels; more important in animals","Releases sebum to waterproof hair"],"e":"<b>Piloerection</b> (arrector pili contraction): air trapped next to the skin acts as insulation – more important in <b>animals</b>, not so much in humans.","w":["Animals.","Not.","Not.","Not."],"k":"Piloerection: insulation (animals)."},{"id":16102,"t":"Structure of the Skin","s":"Name the small dermal muscle responsible for piloerection.","e":"The arrector pili raises the hair, trapping air as insulation (more important in animals).","k":"Arrector pili.","a":["arrector pili","arrector pili muscle","arrectores pilorum"],"rn":[["","What are the 3 parts of the pilosebaceous unit?","What are the 3 parts of the pilosebaceous unit?","Hair follicles; Sebaceous glands; Arrector pili muscle"],["","Arrector Pili","Arrector Pili","Small muscle in the dermis attached to hair follicles; Contraction makes hairs stand on end"],["","Name 3 structures found in the dermis.","Name 3 structures found in the dermis.","Blood vessels; Hair shafts; Arrector pili muscles"]]},{"id":16103,"t":"Structure of the Skin","s":"Which statement about the subcutis is correct?","o":["The fat layer beneath the dermis","The fat layer between epidermis and dermis","The outermost keratin layer","The layer containing the stratum basale","The papillary dermis"],"e":"<b>Sub cutis</b>: fat layer under the dermis (white “bubbles” on H&E; “not cute”).","w":["Beneath.","Corneum.","Epidermis.","Dermis."],"k":"Subcutis = fat.","rn":[["","Image card: What are the 3 layers of skin seen here, top to bottom? How can you tell?","What are the 3 layers of skin seen here, top to bottom? How can you tell?","Epidermis: purple; Dermis: pink; Sub cutis: white bubbly fat"]]},{"id":16104,"t":"Epidermis","s":"Name the layer labelled <b>B</b>, whose keratohyaline granules contain lipid for waterproofing.","e":"B is the stratum granulosum. Breslow thickness is measured from the granular layer to the deepest tumour point.","k":"B = stratum granulosum.","img":{"src":"16/layers","cap":"Epidermis, H&amp;E. Labels removed."},"a":["stratum granulosum","granulosum","granular layer","granular cell layer"],"rn":[["","What do stratum granulosum cells contain?","What do stratum granulosum cells contain?","Keratohyaline granules (basophilic)"],["","How many layers thick is the stratum granulosum?","How many layers thick is the stratum granulosum?","1 to 3 layers"],["","What are the 4 layers of the epidermis (surface to deep)?","What are the 4 layers of the epidermis (surface to deep)?","Stratum corneum (horn cell); Stratum granulosum (granular cell); Stratum spinosum (prickle cell); Stratum basale (basal cell)"]]},{"id":16105,"t":"Skin Cancer","s":"Which explanation best accounts for BCC's appearance as blue on H&E?","o":["It comes from basal cells, already blue","It arises from melanocytes, which stain blue","It contains abundant keratin","It is a vascular tumour","Its cells lack nuclei"],"e":"Because it comes from <b>basal cells</b> (blue anyway), BCC looks <b>very blue</b>: nests of basal cells dropping into the dermis where blue cells should not be.","w":["Basal.","Pink.","Not.","Not."],"k":"BCC blue from basal cells.","rn":[["","What do BCC (basal cell carcinoma) nests look like on histology?","What do BCC (basal cell carcinoma) nests look like on histology?","Nests of blue basal cells; Dropping down into the dermis"],["","Image card: What skin cancer is this? How can you tell?","What skin cancer is this? How can you tell?","Basal cell carcinoma; Blue nests in the dermis; Attached to overlying epidermis"]]},{"id":16106,"t":"Skin Cancer","s":"A slowly growing ulcer on the eyelid of an elderly man invades into the orbit but has not spread to lymph nodes after years. Which term describes this behaviour?","o":["Local infiltration without metastasis","Local infiltration with early metastasis","Distant metastasis","In situ neoplasia","Benign proliferation"],"e":"<b>Infiltrates locally</b> means it can invade adjacent bone or the eye socket from the eyelid; <b>metastasis</b> means cancer cells far from the primary. BCC infiltrates locally but rarely metastasises.","w":["No spread.","Not.","Invades.","Malignant."],"k":"Local infiltration ≠ metastasis."},{"id":16107,"t":"Skin Cancer","s":"Which is a listed risk factor for melanoma but not for SCC?","o":["Crohn's disease","UV radiation","Fair skin","Previous skin cancer","Immunosuppression"],"e":"Melanoma risks include <b>Crohn's disease</b> and HIV/AIDS, sunbeds, &gt;100 moles, family history. UV and fair skin are shared; previous skin cancer and immunosuppression are SCC risks on the slides.","w":["Shared.","Shared.","SCC list.","SCC list."],"k":"Crohn's = melanoma risk.","rn":[["","What are the 10 melanoma risk factors?","What are the 10 melanoma risk factors?","UV radiation; Sunbed use; Skin phototype I/II; Red/blonde hair; Blue/blue-grey eyes; Sunburn; >100 moles; Family history; Crohn's disease; HIV/AIDS"]]},{"id":16108,"t":"Skin Cancer","s":"Which statement about sunbed use and melanoma is correct?","o":["A risk factor even without burning","A risk factor only if you burn","Protective against melanoma","A risk only for SCC","Not mentioned as a risk"],"e":"Melanoma risk factors: <b>sunbed</b> use (you do not need to have burnt).","w":["No burn needed.","Not.","Not.","Listed."],"k":"Sunbeds raise melanoma risk.","rn":[["","Do you need to burn on a sunbed to increase melanoma risk?","Do you need to burn on a sunbed to increase melanoma risk?","No"]]},{"id":16109,"t":"Epidermis","s":"Which epidermal layer would contain melanocytes and Merkel cells?","o":["Stratum basale","Stratum corneum","Stratum granulosum","Stratum lucidum","Subcutis"],"e":"Both <b>melanocytes</b> and <b>Merkel cells</b> are in the <b>basal layer</b>. Langerhans cells are in the mid-upper spinous layer and dermis.","w":["Dead cells.","Not.","Acral clear.","Fat."],"k":"Basale: melanocytes and Merkel cells.","rn":[["","What are the 4 layers of the epidermis (surface to deep)?","What are the 4 layers of the epidermis (surface to deep)?","Stratum corneum (horn cell); Stratum granulosum (granular cell); Stratum spinosum (prickle cell); Stratum basale (basal cell)"],["","How many cells thick is the stratum basale?","How many cells thick is the stratum basale?","A single layer"],["","Image card: Name the 4 layers marked by the arrows, top to bottom.","Name the 4 layers marked by the arrows, top to bottom.","Horn cell layer (stratum corneum); Granular cell layer (stratum granulosum); Prickle cell layer (stratum spinosum); Basal cell layer (stratum basale)"]]},{"id":16110,"t":"Epidermis","s":"In which layer are Langerhans cells found?","o":["Mid–upper spinous layer","Basal layer only, beside melanocytes","Stratum corneum","Subcutis only","Stratum lucidum"],"e":"Langerhans cells: <b>mid to upper spinous layer</b> of the epidermis and <b>dermis</b>; also in other squamous epithelia and lymphoid organs.","w":["Not.","Not.","Not.","Acral."],"k":"Langerhans: mid-upper spinosum.","rn":[["","Where are Langerhans cells found?","Where are Langerhans cells found?","Mid to upper spinous layer and dermis; All squamous epithelium, other epithelium; Lymphoid organs"]]},{"id":16111,"t":"Pathology of the Epidermis","s":"Which lesion is described as looking like a “stuck on cornflake”?","o":["Seborrhoeic keratosis","Psoriasis plaque","BCC","Haemangioma","Keloid"],"e":"<b>Seborrhoeic keratosis</b> looks like a “<b>stuck on cornflake</b>”: brown, warty, very common, benign.","w":["Silvery scale.","Pearly.","Red-purple.","Nodules."],"k":"Seb K = stuck-on cornflake.","rn":[["","Image card: What are these lesions?","What are these lesions?","Seborrhoeic keratoses (seb K / seborrhoeic warts); \"Stuck on cornflake\"; Benign"],["","What do seborrhoeic keratoses look like?","What do seborrhoeic keratoses look like?","Stuck on cornflake"],["","What does seborrhoeic keratosis show on histology?","What does seborrhoeic keratosis show on histology?","Acanthosis; Nests of keratin within the epidermis"]]},{"id":16112,"t":"Skin Cancer","s":"These pigmented lesions are asymmetrical with irregular borders and variable colour, over 6 mm, and changing. Name the diagnosis to exclude.","e":"Melanoma (here superficial spreading). ABCDE: asymmetry, border, colour, diameter >6 mm, evolving. Prognosis depends on Breslow thickness.","k":"Melanoma.","img":{"src":"16/melanoma","cap":"Pigmented lesions."},"a":["melanoma","malignant melanoma","superficial spreading melanoma"],"rn":[["","Image card: What type of melanoma is this, and what is ABCDE?","What type of melanoma is this, and what is ABCDE?","Superficial spreading melanoma; Asymmetry, Border, Colour, Diameter >6 mm, Evolving"]]},{"id":16113,"t":"Epidermis","s":"Which of the following describes the stratum basale cells' shape?","o":["Cuboidal/columnar","Flat and anucleate","Polyhedral and pink","Flattened and granular","Spindle-shaped"],"e":"Basale: single layer, <b>columnar/cuboidal</b>; the slide oddly says “single layer of squamous cells… columnar/cuboidal in shape”, and the recording says cuboidal/columnar.","w":["Corneum.","Spinosum.","Granulosum.","Not."],"k":"Basal cells cuboidal/columnar.","rn":[["","What shape are stratum basale cells?","What shape are stratum basale cells?","Columnar / cuboidal"]]},{"id":16114,"t":"Pathology of the Dermis","s":"Small red-purple raised lesions on the skin and lip show many grouped blood vessels in the dermis. Name the benign tumour.","e":"Haemangioma: a benign tumour of blood vessels in the dermis.","k":"Haemangioma.","img":{"src":"16/haemang","cap":"Small raised red-purple lesions."},"a":["haemangioma","hemangioma","haemangiomas","hemangiomas"],"rn":[["","Image card: What are these red lesions?","What are these red lesions?","Haemangiomas; Benign tumours of blood vessels"],["","Haemangioma","Haemangioma","Benign tumour of blood vessels"],["","Image card: What does this histology show?","What does this histology show?","Haemangioma; Lots of blood vessels grouped in the dermis"]]},{"id":16115,"t":"Dermis","s":"Which labelling of A, B and C is correct?","o":["A epidermis; B dermis; C subcutis","A dermis; B epidermis; C subcutis","A epidermis; B subcutis; C dermis","A basement membrane; B dermis; C subcutis","A epidermis; B dermis; C muscle"],"e":"Epidermis (A): keratinising stratified squamous epithelium. Dermis (B): dense connective tissue supporting the epidermis, with vessels, nerves and appendages. Subcutis (C): fat.","w":["The thin surface layer (A) is the epidermis.","The fat layer (C) is the deepest.","A is the whole thin surface layer.","C contains fat lobules: the subcutis."],"k":"Epidermis, dermis, subcutis.","img":{"src":"16/block","cap":"Block diagram of skin. Labels removed."},"rn":[["","What does the dermis do?","What does the dermis do?","Supports the epidermis"]]},{"id":16116,"t":"Epidermis","s":"Which statement about the stratum spinosum cells' shape is correct?","o":["Polyhedral","Perfectly round (spherical)","Columnar","Anucleate and flat","Spindle-shaped"],"e":"Spinosum cells are <b>polyhedral</b> (about five or six sides; polygonal, not circles); not quite flat lower down because they still have a big nucleus.","w":["Not.","Basal.","Corneum.","Fibroblast."],"k":"Spinous cells polyhedral.","rn":[["","What does polyhedral mean?","What does polyhedral mean?","About 5 or 6 sides"],["","What cells make up the stratum spinosum?","What cells make up the stratum spinosum?","Polyhedral squamous cells (keratinocytes)"]]},{"id":16117,"t":"Skin Cancer","s":"Which explains the thickened keratin of palms and soles?","o":["Acral sites with a lucidum","Sites with more melanocytes","Sites with no dermis","Sites lacking a granular layer","Sites with psoriasis"],"e":"<b>Acral</b> sites (palms, soles) have the stratum lucidum and thickened keratin for protection against <b>friction</b>.","w":["Not.","Not.","Not.","Not."],"k":"Acral: lucidum, friction.","rn":[["","Which sites are at risk in higher skin phototypes?","Which sites are at risk in higher skin phototypes?","Acral sites"]]},{"id":16118,"t":"Skin Cancer","s":"A non-pigmented melanoma may be missed because it does not look dark. Name this variant.","e":"Amelanotic melanoma lacks pigment.","k":"Amelanotic melanoma.","a":["amelanotic melanoma","amelanotic"],"rn":[["","Amelanotic Melanoma","Amelanotic Melanoma","Melanoma that does not produce melanin; Not dark"]]},{"id":16119,"t":"Skin Cancer","s":"A histopathologist sees nests of tumour cells in the dermis; cells are blue, rarely contain keratin, and nests are attached to the epidermis. Nodes are normal. What should she call it and what follow-up concern dominates?","o":["BCC – local recurrence and destruction","BCC – lymph node metastasis","SCC – lymph node metastasis","Melanoma – distant metastasis","Seborrhoeic keratosis – no concern"],"e":"<b>BCC</b>: blue, attached to epidermis, rarely keratin; tends to be <b>multiple</b>, <b>recur</b>, infiltrate locally and be <b>disfiguring</b>, but rarely metastasises.","w":["Rarely.","Pink, keratin.","Atypical melanocytes.","Malignant."],"k":"BCC: recurrence/local destruction."},{"id":16120,"t":"Introduction","s":"What is the most useful way to approach skin disease?","o":["Think by structures affected; structure explains function","Think of skin disease by symptoms alone, not by the structures involved","Only skin cancers really matter","Only the dermis matters","Diagnosis needs no histology"],"e":"Take-home: the 3 main skin cancers are BCC, SCC and melanoma; skin structure is essential to understand function in health and disease; skin diseases can be thought of in terms of the <b>structures affected</b>.","w":["Structure.","Not.","Not.","Not."],"k":"Structure-based approach to skin disease.","rn":[["","How can skin diseases be thought about?","How can skin diseases be thought about?","In terms of the structures affected"]]},{"id":16121,"t":"Skin Cancer","s":"A 45-year-old woman with fair skin, red hair and more than 100 moles notices a mole on her leg that has become asymmetrical, with an irregular border, several colours and a diameter of 8 mm. It is excised. Histology shows highly atypical melanocytes with prominent nucleoli, and the tumour measures 4.5 mm from the granular cell layer to its deepest point.<br><br>What is the diagnosis, and what is her prognosis?","o":["Melanoma; Breslow thickness >4 mm means high metastatic risk and a poor prognosis","Melanoma; Breslow thickness >4 mm means low risk and an excellent prognosis","Basal cell carcinoma; it rarely spreads, so the prognosis is excellent","Squamous cell carcinoma; it makes keratin and only spreads locally","Benign melanoma; the atypical melanocytes mean it can be watched rather than treated as a cancer"],"e":"Step 1: her risk factors (fair skin, red hair, >100 moles), the ABCDE features and atypical melanocytes with prominent nucleoli point to melanoma, which is most common on the trunk and legs. Step 2: prognosis is based on Breslow thickness, measured from the granular cell layer to the deepest point of the tumour: <1 mm has an excellent prognosis, while >4 mm carries a high risk of metastasis and a poor prognosis.","w":["Thickness >4 mm means high, not low, risk.","Atypical melanocytes point to melanoma, not BCC.","SCC arises from squamous cells and can metastasise.","There is no benign melanoma: all types are malignant."],"k":"Breslow: <1 mm excellent; >4 mm poor.","rn":[["","Breslow >4 mm: prognosis?","Breslow >4 mm: prognosis?","High risk of metastasis; Poor prognosis"]]},{"id":16122,"t":"Skin Cancer","s":"Two older men with years of outdoor work each have a lesion on the face. Man A's lesion grows slowly, invades local tissue and has returned after a previous removal, and histology shows \"blue\" cells in the dermis attached to the overlying epithelium. Man B's lesion makes pink keratin and he later develops enlarged lymph nodes.<br><br>What are the two cancers, and which has the worse prognosis?","o":["A: basal cell carcinoma; B: squamous cell carcinoma, which is worse as it can metastasise","A: squamous cell carcinoma; B: basal cell carcinoma, which is worse","A: melanoma; B: basal cell carcinoma","Both basal cell carcinoma, as both are on the face","A: basal cell carcinoma, which is worse because it recurs; B: squamous cell carcinoma, which rarely spreads"],"e":"Step 1: basal cell carcinoma (BCC) is the most common skin cancer, often on the head and neck, infiltrates locally, tends to recur and appears as \"blue\" cells in the dermis attached to the overlying epithelium. Step 2: squamous cell carcinoma (SCC) makes keratin and, unlike BCC, can metastasise to lymph nodes, lung, liver and brain, so its prognosis is worse than BCC.","w":["The blue cells attached to epithelium are BCC, and BCC has the best prognosis.","Man A has no atypical melanocytes, and man B makes keratin.","Man B's lesion makes keratin and spreads to nodes, which fits SCC.","BCC rarely spreads; SCC can metastasise."],"k":"BCC: local, blue, recurs; SCC: keratin, metastasises → worse.","rn":[["","What are the 3 main types of primary skin cancer?","What are the 3 main types of primary skin cancer?","Squamous cell carcinoma; Basal cell carcinoma; Melanoma"],["","Image card: Which skin cancer is each? (left, middle, right)","Which skin cancer is each? (left, middle, right)","Left: squamous cell carcinoma (pink, keratin); Middle: basal cell carcinoma (blue); Right: melanoma (brown pigment)"],["","Image card: What skin cancer is this? How can you tell?","What skin cancer is this? How can you tell?","Basal cell carcinoma; Blue nests in the dermis; Attached to overlying epidermis"]]},{"id":16123,"t":"Epidermis","s":"A biopsy of a skin lesion shows abnormal squamous cells filling the full thickness of the epidermis. In one part of the specimen, the cells have pushed through the layer of type IV collagen beneath the epidermis into the dermis. The pathologist changes the diagnosis on the report.<br><br>What does breaching this layer mean?","o":["The basement membrane is breached, so the lesion is now invasive, not in situ","The stratum corneum is breached, so the lesion is benign","The hemidesmosomes have formed, so the lesion is healing","The dermis is breached, so the lesion is a keloid scar","The basement membrane is breached, but this has no effect on the diagnosis or on spread"],"e":"Step 1: the basement membrane, made of type IV collagen, resists shearing forces and controls epithelial–mesenchymal interactions such as invasion. Step 2: breach of the basement membrane means invasion, so the lesion moves from in situ to invasive carcinoma.","w":["The breach is of the basement membrane, and it means invasion.","Hemidesmosomes anchor basal cells; this is not healing.","A keloid is a dermal scar, not epithelial invasion.","Breaching the basement membrane changes in situ to invasive."],"k":"Basement membrane (type IV collagen) breach = invasion.","rn":[["","What happens if atypical cells breach the basement membrane?","What happens if atypical cells breach the basement membrane?","Invasion; In situ becomes invasive cancer"]]},{"id":16124,"t":"Function of the Skin","s":"A 25-year-old runner finishes a marathon on a hot day. Her skin is flushed and she is sweating heavily. Later, she sits outside in the cold and her skin becomes pale and her body hair stands up. Her coach asks how her skin helped control her temperature in each setting.<br><br>Which mechanisms were used?","o":["Heat: vasodilation and sweating; cold: vasoconstriction and piloerection","Heat: vasoconstriction and piloerection; cold: vasodilation and sweating","Heat: piloerection; cold: sweating","Heat: vasodilation only; cold: sweating to warm the skin by evaporation","Both: the skin plays no part, as only the hypothalamus controls temperature"],"e":"Step 1: to lose heat, cutaneous blood flow increases (vasodilation), losing heat by conduction and convection, and sweating gives evaporative cooling through the latent heat of vaporisation. Step 2: to conserve heat, cutaneous blood flow decreases (vasoconstriction), and piloerection traps air next to the skin as insulation (more important in animals).","w":["The mechanisms are reversed.","Piloerection conserves heat and sweating loses it.","Sweating loses heat; it does not warm the skin.","The skin plays a major role in thermoregulation alongside the hypothalamus."],"k":"Lose heat: vasodilation + sweat; conserve: vasoconstriction + piloerection.","rn":[["","How does increased blood flow help?","How does increased blood flow help?","Vasodilation; Heat loss by conduction and convection"],["","How does decreased blood flow help?","How does decreased blood flow help?","Vasoconstriction; Heat conservation"],["","How does sweating cool you?","How does sweating cool you?","Evaporative cooling; Latent heat of vaporisation"]]},{"id":16125,"t":"Skin Cancer","s":"A pathologist examines skin from the sole of the foot. Starting deepest, she sees a single layer of blue columnar cells anchored to the basement membrane, then a thick pink layer joined by prickle-like connections, then flattened cells with dark granules that act as waterproofing. Above this is a clear layer and finally a layer of cells with no nuclei in a basket-weave pattern.<br><br>Which layer is the clear layer, and why is it present in this sample?","o":["Stratum lucidum; it is found only at acral sites such as the palms and soles","Stratum granulosum; it is found in all skin","Stratum spinosum; it is clear because of desmosomes","Stratum basale; it is clear because of melanin","Stratum corneum; it is clear because the cells still have their nuclei"],"e":"Step 1: from deep to superficial, the layers are stratum basale (blue, single layer, hemidesmosomes), spinosum (pink bulk, desmosomes), granulosum (keratohyaline granules with lipid for waterproofing), lucidum and corneum (no nuclei, basket-weave keratin). Step 2: the stratum lucidum, a clear layer of dead keratinocytes, is found only in acral sites (palms and soles), which is why it appears in a sole sample.","w":["The granulosum has dark granules, not a clear appearance.","The spinosum is pink with prickles, not clear.","The basale is a single blue layer at the base.","The corneum has no nuclei and lies above the lucidum."],"k":"Stratum lucidum: clear layer only at acral sites.","rn":[["","Acral","Acral","Palms and soles"],["","Why do palms and soles have a stratum lucidum?","Why do palms and soles have a stratum lucidum?","Exposed to a lot of friction; Need extra protection"],["","Image card: What type of melanoma is this? Where is it?","What type of melanoma is this? Where is it?","Acral lentiginous melanoma; Palms and soles"]]},{"id":16126,"t":"Epidermis","s":"A researcher studies cells in the basal layer of the epidermis. Cell type 1 is a dendritic cell from the neural crest, seen on H&E as an elongated nucleus with a clear halo, about one for every four basal cells. Cell type 2, also from the neural crest and in the basal layer, cannot be seen on H&E and is common in the fingertips and lips.<br><br>What are the cells, and what does each do?","o":["1: melanocyte, makes melanin; 2: Merkel cell, a slowly adapting mechanoreceptor for touch","1: Langerhans cell, presents antigen; 2: melanocyte, makes melanin","1: keratinocyte, gives structure; 2: Langerhans cell, presents antigen","1: Merkel cell, senses touch; 2: melanocyte, produces pigment","1: fibroblast, makes collagen; 2: histiocyte, removes debris by phagocytosis in the dermis"],"e":"Step 1: melanocytes are dendritic cells from the neural crest in the basal layer, about 1 in every 4 basal cells, seen as elongated nuclei with a clear halo; they produce melanin in melanosomes that pass to keratinocytes. Step 2: Merkel cells, also from the neural crest and in the basal layer, are slowly adapting mechanoreceptors for tactile sensation, increased in digits and lips, and cannot be seen on H&E.","w":["Langerhans cells are in the mid–upper spinous layer and are not neural-crest touch cells.","Keratinocytes are epithelial, not neural crest.","The two cells are swapped.","Fibroblasts and histiocytes are dermal cells."],"k":"Melanocyte: neural crest, halo, 1:4; Merkel: neural crest, touch, not seen on H&E.","rn":[["","What is Merkel Cells?","What is Merkel Cells?","Slowly adapting mechanoreceptors; For tactile sensation"]]},{"id":16127,"t":"Dermis","s":"A 16-year-old boy complains of body odour from his armpits since puberty. His doctor explains that two kinds of sweat glands exist. The ones found all over the body help control temperature, while the ones in the armpit, groin and nipple are different in structure and function.<br><br>Which glands cause his odour, and how do they look on histology?","o":["Apocrine glands; pinker columnar cells with \"decapitation\" secretion","Eccrine glands; bluer cuboidal cells with myoepithelial cells","Sebaceous glands; foamy sebocytes with lipid","Eccrine glands; columnar cells with decapitation secretion","Apocrine glands; foamy lipid-filled cells that drain into the hair follicle at the isthmus"],"e":"Step 1: eccrine sweat glands are found all over the body and control temperature, while apocrine glands are in specific locations (axilla, groin, nipple) and are responsible for body odour. Step 2: apocrine glands have columnar cells with \"decapitation\" secretion and look pinker, whereas eccrine glands have cuboidal cells surrounded by myoepithelial cells and look bluer.","w":["Eccrine glands are for temperature control.","Sebaceous glands make lipid secretions, not body odour sweat.","Eccrine glands have cuboidal cells and look bluer.","Foamy lipid cells draining into follicles are sebaceous glands."],"k":"Apocrine: axilla/groin, odour, pink decapitation; eccrine: everywhere, temperature, blue.","rn":[["","What cells make apocrine glands?","What cells make apocrine glands?","Columnar cells; With \"decapitation\" secretion"],["","Image card: What type of sweat gland is this? How can you tell?","What type of sweat gland is this? How can you tell?","Apocrine; Pink columnar cells; Decapitation secretion (sweat in the lumen)"]]},{"id":16128,"t":"Pathology of the Dermis","s":"Two patients develop raised scars after surgery. In patient 1, the raised scar stays within the margins of the original wound. In patient 2, firm smooth nodules extend well beyond the original cut, and histology shows thick, haphazard, rope-like collagen bundles.<br><br>Which scars are these, and in which skin layer is the problem?","o":["1: hypertrophic scar; 2: keloid scar; both are dermal conditions","1: keloid; 2: hypertrophic scar; both are epidermal","Both are keloids; both are epidermal","1: hypertrophic scar; 2: haemangioma; both are in the sub cutis","1: psoriasis; 2: keloid; both are blistering conditions of the epidermis"],"e":"Step 1: a hypertrophic scar does not extend beyond the margin of the initial wound, while a keloid scar extends beyond the original injury, forming firm smooth nodules with thick, haphazard, rope-like collagen. Step 2: both involve collagen in the dermis, so they are dermal conditions.","w":["The scars are swapped, and collagen scars are dermal.","Only scar 2 extends beyond the wound.","A haemangioma is a vascular dermal lesion.","Psoriasis is epidermal and not a scar."],"k":"Hypertrophic: within margin; keloid: beyond margin; both dermal.","rn":[["","Keloid Scar","Keloid Scar","Scar tissue extending beyond the margin of the original injury; Firm, smooth nodules"],["","Image card: What 2 types of scar are these (top vs bottom)?","What 2 types of scar are these (top vs bottom)?","Top: hypertrophic (within the wound margin); Bottom: keloid (beyond the margin)"],["","Hypertrophic Scar","Hypertrophic Scar","Scar tissue that does not extend beyond the margin of the initial wound"]]},{"id":16129,"t":"Introduction","s":"A doctor sees four patients with skin findings. Patient 1 has a butterfly-shaped rash across the cheeks. Patient 2 has severe generalised itching that will not settle. Patient 3 has splinter haemorrhages under the nails. Patient 4 has an itchy blistering rash and gets diarrhoea after eating bread.<br><br>Which systemic illness is each finding linked to?","o":["1: lupus; 2: chronic renal failure; 3: bacterial endocarditis/vasculitis; 4: coeliac disease","1: coeliac disease; 2: lupus; 3: chronic renal failure; 4: endocarditis","1: lupus; 2: coeliac disease; 3: chronic renal failure; 4: vasculitis","1: endocarditis; 2: vasculitis; 3: lupus; 4: renal failure","All four are primary skin disorders with no link to systemic illness"],"e":"Step 1: cutaneous manifestations of systemic illness include the malar (butterfly) rash of lupus and generalised intractable pruritus of chronic renal failure. Step 2: splinter haemorrhages are linked to bacterial endocarditis or vasculitis, and dermatitis herpetiformis is linked to coeliac disease (gluten sensitivity).","w":["The butterfly rash is lupus, not coeliac disease.","Intractable itch is linked to renal failure.","The butterfly rash is lupus, not endocarditis.","The lecture links each to a systemic illness."],"k":"Malar rash: lupus; pruritus: renal failure; splinter: endocarditis; DH: coeliac.","rn":[["","What are the 5 cutaneous signs of systemic illness listed?","What are the 5 cutaneous signs of systemic illness listed?","Malar/butterfly rash — lupus; Intractable pruritus. — chronic renal failure; Splinter haemorrhages — endocarditis/vasculitis; Half and half nails"]]},{"id":16130,"t":"Dermis","s":"A 50-year-old man has a skin wound that is healing. A biopsy of the dermis shows many spindle-shaped cells making new collagen and extracellular matrix. It also shows larger cells with bean-shaped nuclei that are engulfing debris; some have fused into giant cells. These larger cells came from the blood.<br><br>What are the two cell types, and how do they differ?","o":["Fibroblasts (not terminally differentiated, make ECM) and histiocytes (from monocytes, phagocytic)","Histiocytes (make collagen) and fibroblasts (from monocytes)","Keratinocytes (make collagen) and melanocytes (phagocytic)","Fibroblasts (fully differentiated) and Langerhans cells (make ECM)","Merkel cells (make collagen) and mast cells, which are the most common connective tissue cell"],"e":"Step 1: fibroblasts are spindle-shaped, not terminally differentiated, the most common connective tissue cell, and synthesise extracellular matrix and collagen, with roles in inflammation, wound healing and angiogenesis. Step 2: histiocytes are fully differentiated, migrate from the bone marrow as monocytes in the blood, have bean-shaped nuclei, can form giant cells and present antigen and remove debris by phagocytosis.","w":["The roles are swapped.","Keratinocytes and melanocytes are epidermal cells.","Fibroblasts are not terminally differentiated.","Merkel cells are touch receptors; fibroblasts are the most common connective tissue cell."],"k":"Fibroblast: spindle, ECM/collagen; histiocyte: from monocytes, phagocytic, giant cells.","rn":[["","Fibroblasts vs histiocytes: differentiation","Fibroblasts vs histiocytes: differentiation","Fibroblasts: not terminally differentiated; Histiocytes: fully differentiated"]]}]);
